# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Hjulstrom
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Sediment transport and the Hjulström diagram",
    title="Moving grains: flow, settling and the Hjulström diagram",
    slug="gyu1-sediment-transport-hjulstrom",
    minutes=24,
    summary="How water moves sediment: laminar and turbulent flow, traction, saltation, suspension and solution, Stokes' law of settling, and how to read and evaluate the Hjulström diagram of erosion, transport and deposition.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish laminar and turbulent flow and the four modes of sediment transport",
        "Use Stokes' law to calculate and compare settling velocities of fine grains",
        "Read the Hjulström diagram to predict erosion, transport or deposition of a grain",
        "Explain the shape of the erosion curve and evaluate the limits of the diagram",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Laminar flow", "Turbulent flow", "Bed load", "Suspended load", "Saltation", "Stokes' law",
                  "Critical erosion velocity", "Settling velocity", "Hjulström diagram", "Cohesion"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("After a heavy storm the River Mungo turns brown with mud and you can hear pebbles knocking on its bed. A week later the water is clear, yet the pebbles have stopped on a gravel bar and the mud has settled in quiet pools. Every sedimentary rock starts with grains that were picked up, carried and dropped like this. This lesson explains **why** a given flow erodes some grains, carries others and drops the rest."),
        D("In **laminar flow** the fluid moves in smooth parallel layers that do not mix. In **turbulent flow** the fluid swirls in eddies that mix it up and down. Almost all rivers, waves and wind are turbulent; laminar flow is found only in a very thin layer next to the bed (the viscous sublayer) and in very slow, shallow or very viscous flows such as mudflows.", title="Laminar and turbulent flow"),
        T("Turbulence matters because the upward-moving eddies lift fine grains and keep them in the water. The **Reynolds number** compares the forces that drive the flow with the viscous forces that damp it; a large value means turbulent flow."),
        F(r"Re = \frac{\rho\, u\, L}{\mu}", title="Reynolds number (rho density, u velocity, L depth, mu viscosity)"),
        KEY("Four modes of transport", "A river's load is carried in four ways. Name the mode and the size of grain that travels that way.", cards=[
            ["Traction (bed load)", "Pebbles and boulders roll or slide along the bed. Only moved at high velocity, for example in floods."],
            ["Saltation (bed load)", "Sand grains are lifted, carried a short way and fall back, so they move in short hops."],
            ["Suspension (suspended load)", "Silt and clay are held up by turbulent eddies and move with the water. This gives the brown colour of a river in flood."],
            ["Solution (dissolved load)", "Ions such as calcium and hydrogencarbonate travel dissolved in the water and are invisible."]]),
        H("How fast do grains settle?"),
        T("When the flow slows, a grain falls at its **settling (fall) velocity**. For small grains (below about 0.1 mm) falling slowly through still water, the drag balances the grain's weight minus buoyancy, which gives Stokes' law:"),
        F(r"v = \frac{(\rho_s - \rho_f)\, g\, d^{2}}{18\,\mu}", title="Stokes' law (rho_s grain density, rho_f fluid density, d grain diameter)"),
        T("The key point is the $d^2$: halve the diameter and the grain settles four times more slowly. That is why clay stays in suspension for days and is deposited only in very quiet water such as lakes, lagoons and the deep sea."),
        EX("Worked example 1: Stokes' law", intro="A quartz silt grain ($\\rho_s$ = 2 650 kg/m3) of diameter 0.01 mm settles through still water ($\\rho_f$ = 1 000 kg/m3, $\\mu$ = 0.001 Pa s, g = 9.81 m/s2). Find its settling velocity and the time it takes to fall 1 m.", steps=[
            S("Convert the diameter to metres", math=r"d = 0.01\text{ mm} = 1.0 \times 10^{-5}\text{ m}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"v = \frac{(2650 - 1000)(9.81)(1.0\times10^{-5})^{2}}{18 \times 0.001}"),
            S("Calculate", math=r"v = 9.0 \times 10^{-5}\text{ m/s} \approx 0.09\text{ mm/s}"),
            S("Time to fall 1 m", math=r"t = \frac{1}{9.0\times10^{-5}} \approx 1.1\times10^{4}\text{ s} \approx 3.1\text{ hours}"),
        ], check=r"1650 \times 9.81 \times 10^{-10} / 0.018 = 8.99\times10^{-5}"),
        H("The Hjulström diagram"),
        T("In 1935 the Swedish geographer Filip Hjulström plotted, for well-sorted quartz grains in water about 1 m deep, the mean flow velocity against grain diameter. Both axes are logarithmic because both quantities vary over many powers of ten."),
        IMG("algyu1-hjulstrom.svg", alt="Log-log graph of velocity against grain size. A solid erosion curve has a minimum near 0.3 mm and rises for finer and coarser grains. A dashed settling curve lies below it. Fields: erosion above, transport between, deposition below.", caption="Figure 1. A schematic Hjulström diagram. Exact values differ between textbooks: read the shape, not precise numbers."),
        KEY("Reading the diagram", "Find the grain size on the horizontal axis, go up to the flow velocity and see which field you are in.", cards=[
            ["Above the erosion curve", "A grain lying on the bed is picked up (eroded)."],
            ["Between the curves", "A grain already moving keeps moving (transport), but a grain at rest on the bed is not picked up."],
            ["Below the settling curve", "A moving grain is dropped (deposition)."]]),
        T("The erosion curve has a minimum for fine to medium sand (about 0.1 to 0.5 mm). Coarser grains need more velocity because they are heavier. Finer grains, surprisingly, also need more velocity. Clay and fine silt particles are **cohesive**: flat clay flakes stick together by electrostatic forces, and the small grains lie inside the slow viscous sublayer, so the bed is smooth and the turbulent eddies cannot grip them. Once eroded, however, they settle so slowly that they stay in suspension even at very low velocity, so the gap between the curves is widest for fine grains."),
        EX("Worked example 2: reading the diagram", intro="Using Figure 1, a flood flowing at 100 cm/s slows to 20 cm/s. Describe what happens to (a) gravel of 10 mm, (b) sand of 0.3 mm and (c) clay already in suspension.", steps=[
            S("Gravel at 100 cm/s", text="The point lies on the erosion curve, so 10 mm gravel is only just moved by the flood."),
            S("Gravel at 20 cm/s", text="This is below the settling curve for 10 mm (about 70 cm/s), so the gravel is deposited first, as a gravel bar."),
            S("Sand", text="At 100 cm/s sand is eroded (far above the minimum). At 20 cm/s it is between the curves: sand already moving keeps moving, so it is carried further and deposited later."),
            S("Clay", text="At 20 cm/s clay is well above its settling curve, so it stays in suspension and is carried right out to a lake or the sea."),
            S("Conclusion", text="As a flow slows, sediment is sorted by size: gravel is dropped first, then sand, and clay last."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: comparing settling", intro="By Stokes' law, how many times faster does a 0.004 mm grain settle than a 0.002 mm grain of the same mineral?", steps=[
            S("Use the d squared rule", math=r"\frac{v_1}{v_2} = \left(\frac{d_1}{d_2}\right)^{2} = \left(\frac{0.004}{0.002}\right)^{2}"),
            S("Answer", math=r"= 2^{2} = 4"),
        ], check=r"(0.004/0.002)^2 = 4"),
        KEY("Evaluating the Hjulström diagram", "Essay questions often ask you to assess a model. Give strengths and limits.", cards=[
            ["Strengths", "Simple; explains sorting by size, the hysteresis between picking up and dropping grains, and why fine sand is the easiest sediment to erode."],
            ["Limits", "Based on mean velocity, not the shear stress on the bed, so it changes with flow depth. It assumes quartz grains of one size and shape; flat mica flakes, dense grains such as magnetite or a consolidated bed behave differently."],
            ["Better approach", "Sedimentologists often use the Shields criterion, which relates the bed shear stress to grain size and density. Check your textbook for how far your syllabus goes."]]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up erosion and transport processes", "Abrasion, attrition, hydraulic action, cavitation and corrosion (solution) are processes of **erosion**. Traction, saltation, suspension and solution are modes of **transport**. Examiners reported that candidates confused the two lists and did not say which particle sizes each process moves."),
        MISTAKE("Saying clay is eroded at the lowest velocity", "The minimum of the erosion curve is for fine to medium sand. Clay needs a higher velocity because of cohesion, even though it settles most slowly."),
        QUIZ("A grain of 0.3 mm sand lies on a river bed. The flow velocity is just above its settling curve but well below its erosion curve. What happens?", ["It is eroded", "It stays on the bed", "It dissolves", "It is carried in suspension"], 1, "Between the curves only grains already moving keep moving. A grain at rest is not picked up.", hint="Is the grain at rest or moving?", feedback=["Erosion needs a velocity above the erosion curve.", "", "Quartz sand does not dissolve in river water on this time scale.", "Sand of 0.3 mm is not held in suspension at such a low velocity, and it is not even moving."]),
        QUIZ("Why does fine clay need a higher velocity to be eroded than fine sand?", ["Clay is denser than sand", "Clay particles are cohesive and lie in the smooth viscous sublayer", "Clay settles faster than sand", "Clay is carried only by traction"], 1, "Cohesion and a smooth bed resist erosion of fine particles.", hint="Think about how clay particles stick together.", feedback=["Clay minerals have a density similar to quartz.", "", "Clay settles much more slowly (Stokes' law).", "Clay travels in suspension, not by traction."]),
        TIP("Hjulström diagrams and Stokes' law can appear in Paper 1 as diagram questions, and sediment transport and sorting feed essays in Paper 2 (for example on river processes and on the environment of a sedimentary rock). In Paper 3 you may be asked to relate grain size and sorting of a specimen to the energy of the transporting current.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Distinguish between bed load and suspended load and name the mode by which each is moved.", "Bed load is the coarser sediment that moves in contact with or close to the bed, by traction (rolling and sliding of pebbles and boulders) and saltation (sand moving in hops). Suspended load is fine silt and clay held up in the water by turbulent eddies and carried at the speed of the flow."),
        PQ("Calculate the settling velocity of a 0.02 mm quartz grain in still water, using Stokes' law with grain density 2 650 kg/m3, water density 1 000 kg/m3, viscosity 0.001 Pa s and g = 9.81 m/s2.", "v = (2650 - 1000) x 9.81 x (2.0 x 10^-5)^2 / (18 x 0.001) = 3.6 x 10^-4 m/s, about 0.36 mm/s. (It is four times the value for a 0.01 mm grain because velocity depends on d squared.)"),
        PQ("Explain the shape of the critical erosion velocity curve on the Hjulström diagram.", "The curve has a minimum for fine to medium sand (about 0.1 to 0.5 mm). For coarser grains the velocity rises because larger grains are heavier and need more force to lift or roll. For finer grains the velocity also rises because silt and clay particles are cohesive (clay flakes are held by electrostatic attraction), the bed they form is smooth, and they lie within the viscous sublayer where the turbulent eddies cannot reach them."),
        PQ("Essay plan: 'The Hjulström diagram explains how rivers sort their sediment.' Discuss.", "Plan: (1) Define the diagram: mean velocity against grain size, log axes, erosion and settling curves, three fields. (2) Explain sorting: as velocity falls, grains are deposited in order of settling velocity: gravel first, then sand, then silt and clay, giving gravel bars, sand bars and floodplain muds. (3) Explain hysteresis: fine grains, once moving, stay suspended at low velocity. (4) Evaluation: based on mean velocity for about 1 m depth and uniform quartz grains; does not include grain shape, density, depth, bed consolidation or turbulence; Shields criterion is more general. (5) Conclusion: a useful first model of sorting, but real deposits also reflect supply of sediment and flow history."),
        SUM("Rivers are turbulent; eddies keep fine grains in suspension.",
            "Load moves by traction, saltation, suspension and solution; do not confuse these with erosion processes.",
            "Stokes' law: settling velocity is proportional to d squared, so clay settles very slowly.",
            "Hjulström diagram: erosion above the solid curve, transport between the curves, deposition below the settling curve.",
            "Fine sand is easiest to erode; clay is hard to erode because of cohesion but easy to keep moving.",
            "Limits: mean velocity, fixed depth, quartz grains of one size; the Shields criterion is more general."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Grain size analysis
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Grain size analysis",
    title="Grain size analysis: the phi scale and Folk and Ward statistics",
    slug="gyu1-grain-size-analysis-phi-statistics",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Measuring and describing grain size: the Wentworth scale and phi units, sieve analysis and cumulative curves, and the Folk and Ward graphic mean, sorting and skewness used to interpret the energy and environment of a sediment.",
    objectives=[
        "Convert grain diameters to phi units and name Wentworth size classes",
        "Turn sieve data into a cumulative frequency curve and read percentiles",
        "Calculate the Folk and Ward graphic mean, sorting and skewness and describe the result",
        "Use grain size statistics, with care, to interpret the energy and environment of deposition",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Wentworth scale", "Phi scale", "Sieve analysis", "Cumulative frequency curve", "Graphic mean",
                  "Sorting", "Skewness", "Textural maturity"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Grain size is the most basic property of a clastic sediment. It tells you how much energy the current had (from the Hjulström diagram), and the spread of sizes tells you how long and how steadily it worked on the grains. Sand from a beach at Limbe or Kribi, from a river bar and from a desert dune can look similar in the hand, but their grain size statistics are often different. This lesson shows how geologists measure and describe grain size."),
        D("The **Wentworth scale** divides clastic grains into classes, each class twice the size of the one below: clay (below 1/256 mm), silt (1/256 to 1/16 mm), sand (1/16 to 2 mm), granules (2 to 4 mm), pebbles (4 to 64 mm), cobbles (64 to 256 mm) and boulders (above 256 mm).", title="Wentworth grade scale"),
        D("Because each class doubles, Krumbein's **phi scale** uses logarithms to base 2. Large grains have negative phi values and fine grains positive values.", title="Phi units", formula=r"\phi = -\log_{2} d \qquad d = 2^{-\phi}\ (d \text{ in mm})"),
        T("So 1 mm is 0 phi, 2 mm is -1 phi, 0.5 mm is 1 phi, 0.25 mm is 2 phi and 1/16 mm (0.0625 mm, the sand-silt boundary) is 4 phi. Sand therefore runs from -1 to 4 phi. Phi units turn the doubling scale into equal steps, so ordinary statistics can be used."),
        KEY("Measuring grain size", "The method depends on the size of the grains.", cards=[
            ["Gravel", "Measure the long, intermediate and short axes of individual clasts with a ruler or callipers."],
            ["Sand", "Sieve analysis: shake a dry, weighed sample through a stack of sieves at half or whole phi intervals and weigh what each sieve retains."],
            ["Silt and clay", "Settling methods (pipette or hydrometer), which use Stokes' law, or laser diffraction instruments."],
            ["Thin sections", "Measure grains under the microscope; useful for lithified rocks that cannot be disaggregated."]]),
        H("From sieve data to statistics"),
        T("Convert each sieve weight to a percentage of the total, then add them up from the coarsest sieve to give the **cumulative percentage coarser** than each sieve size. Plot this against phi to get the cumulative frequency curve and read off the phi values at chosen percentiles: phi5, phi16, phi50, phi84 and phi95."),
        CODE("Sieve (phi)  Size (mm)  Retained (g)  Cumulative % coarser\n    0          1.00         5              5\n    1          0.50        11             16\n    2          0.25        34             50\n    3          0.125       34             84\n    4          0.0625      11             95\n  pan          <0.0625      5            100\nTotal                     100 g", "text", title="Table 1. Sieve analysis of a sand (invented for practice)"),
        IMG("algyu1-cumcurve.svg", alt="Cumulative curve of percentage coarser against phi from -1 to 4, through 0 at -1, 5 at 0, 16 at 1, 50 at 2, 84 at 3 and 95 at 4, with dashed lines reading phi16 = 1.0, phi50 = 2.0 and phi84 = 3.0.", caption="Figure 1. Cumulative curve for Table 1, with straight lines between sieve points. Many textbooks use probability paper, which straightens the curve; the reading method is the same."),
        KEY("Folk and Ward (1957) graphic measures", "The inclusive graphic measures use five percentiles.", cards=[
            ["Graphic mean", r"$M_z = (\phi_{16} + \phi_{50} + \phi_{84})/3$: the average size, in phi."],
            ["Inclusive graphic standard deviation (sorting)", r"$\sigma_I = \frac{\phi_{84} - \phi_{16}}{4} + \frac{\phi_{95} - \phi_{5}}{6.6}$. Under 0.35 very well sorted; 0.35-0.50 well; 0.50-0.71 moderately well; 0.71-1.00 moderately; 1.00-2.00 poorly; 2.00-4.00 very poorly sorted."],
            ["Inclusive graphic skewness", r"$Sk_I = \frac{\phi_{16} + \phi_{84} - 2\phi_{50}}{2(\phi_{84} - \phi_{16})} + \frac{\phi_{5} + \phi_{95} - 2\phi_{50}}{2(\phi_{95} - \phi_{5})}$. Positive: tail of fine grains (fine-skewed). Negative: tail of coarse grains (coarse-skewed). Between -0.1 and +0.1: nearly symmetrical."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: statistics from Table 1", intro="From Figure 1 and Table 1: phi5 = 0, phi16 = 1.0, phi50 = 2.0, phi84 = 3.0, phi95 = 4.0. Calculate and describe the mean, sorting and skewness.", steps=[
            S("Graphic mean", math=r"M_z = \frac{1.0 + 2.0 + 3.0}{3} = 2.0\,\phi"),
            S("Convert to mm", math=r"d = 2^{-2.0} = 0.25\text{ mm (boundary of medium and fine sand)}"),
            S("Sorting", math=r"\sigma_I = \frac{3.0 - 1.0}{4} + \frac{4.0 - 0}{6.6} = 0.50 + 0.61 = 1.11"),
            S("Describe sorting", text="1.11 lies between 1.00 and 2.00: poorly sorted."),
            S("Skewness", math=r"Sk_I = \frac{1 + 3 - 4}{4} + \frac{0 + 4 - 4}{8} = 0"),
            S("Interpret", text="A poorly sorted, symmetrical medium to fine sand: deposited quickly, with little reworking, for example by a river in flood."),
        ], check=r"0.5 + 4/6.6 = 1.106"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a beach sand", intro="A beach sand gives phi5 = 0.5, phi16 = 1.2, phi50 = 1.8, phi84 = 2.2, phi95 = 2.6. Find the sorting and skewness.", steps=[
            S("Sorting", math=r"\sigma_I = \frac{2.2 - 1.2}{4} + \frac{2.6 - 0.5}{6.6} = 0.25 + 0.32 = 0.57"),
            S("Describe", text="Between 0.50 and 0.71: moderately well sorted."),
            S("Skewness, first term", math=r"\frac{1.2 + 2.2 - 3.6}{2(1.0)} = -0.10"),
            S("Skewness, second term", math=r"\frac{0.5 + 2.6 - 3.6}{2(2.1)} = -0.12"),
            S("Total", math=r"Sk_I = -0.22 \text{ (coarse-skewed)}"),
            S("Interpret", text="Waves washing back and forth winnow out the fine grains, leaving a tail of coarse grains. Coarse skew is common in beach sands, while many dune sands are fine-skewed, but the overlap is large."),
        ], check=r"-0.1 - 0.5/4.2 = -0.219"),
        KEY("What the statistics mean", "Always link the numbers to process.", cards=[
            ["Mean size", "Energy of the current: coarser mean, higher energy."],
            ["Sorting", "How constant the energy was and how long the sediment was reworked. Wind and beach swash sort well; glaciers, mudflows and flash floods sort poorly."],
            ["Skewness", "Whether fines were removed (coarse skew, winnowing) or added (fine skew, for example deposition from suspension or a low-energy setting)."],
            ["Textural maturity (Folk)", "Immature: much clay matrix. Submature: little matrix but poorly sorted. Mature: well sorted, grains not rounded. Supermature: well sorted and well rounded."]]),
        T("Evaluation: plots such as skewness against sorting have been used to separate beach, dune and river sands, and they often work for modern sands in one region. But the fields overlap, the source of the sediment controls which sizes are available, and diagenesis can change the sizes in an ancient rock. Use grain size together with sedimentary structures, fossils and geometry."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the minus sign in phi", "Phi is minus log base 2 of the diameter. Coarse grains have negative phi. A 4 mm granule is -2 phi, not +2 phi."),
        MISTAKE("Writing 'coarse grain' instead of 'coarse grained'", "The Board's examiners noted candidates who wrote 'coarse grain' or 'fine grain' when describing the texture of a rock. Write coarse grained, medium grained, fine grained, and give sorting, shape and maturity as well."),
        QUIZ("A grain has a diameter of 0.125 mm. Its size in phi units is", ["-3", "3", "0.125", "8"], 1, "0.125 = 2 to the power -3, so phi = 3 (very fine sand boundary).", hint="Write 0.125 as a power of 2.", feedback=["The minus sign belongs to log d; for d below 1 mm phi is positive.", "", "That is the size in mm, not in phi.", "8 is 1/0.125, not the logarithm."]),
        QUIZ("Sand A has an inclusive graphic standard deviation of 0.30; sand B has 1.40. Which is correct?", ["A is poorly sorted", "B is well sorted", "A is very well sorted and B poorly sorted", "Both are moderately sorted"], 2, "Below 0.35 is very well sorted; 1.00 to 2.00 is poorly sorted.", hint="A larger standard deviation means a wider spread of sizes.", feedback=["0.30 is the very well sorted class.", "1.40 is poorly sorted: a wide spread of sizes.", "", "Moderately sorted is 0.71 to 1.00."]),
        TIP("Grain size, sorting, rounding and maturity are part of every description of a sedimentary rock in Paper 3 and in Paper 2 essays on sandstones. Phi conversions and the reading of cumulative curves suit Paper 1. Always describe texture with 'grained' and include sorting and maturity.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Convert (a) 4 mm, (b) 0.0625 mm and (c) 0.35 mm to phi units.", "(a) 4 = 2^2, so phi = -2. (b) 0.0625 = 2^-4, so phi = 4 (the sand-silt boundary). (c) phi = -log2(0.35) = 1.51, so about 1.5 phi (medium sand)."),
        PQ("A sieve analysis gives these weights retained on the 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 phi sieves and the pan: 1.6, 6.4, 20, 32, 16 and 4 g. What percentage of the sample is coarser than 3 phi?", "Total = 80 g. Coarser than 3 phi = 1.6 + 6.4 + 20 + 32 = 60 g. Percentage = 60/80 x 100 = 75 per cent."),
        PQ("A sand gives phi5 = 1.2, phi16 = 1.6, phi50 = 2.2, phi84 = 3.0 and phi95 = 3.8. Calculate the graphic mean, sorting and skewness and describe the sand.", "Mean = (1.6 + 2.2 + 3.0)/3 = 2.27 phi (fine sand, about 0.21 mm). Sorting = 1.4/4 + 2.6/6.6 = 0.35 + 0.39 = 0.74: moderately sorted. Skewness = (1.6 + 3.0 - 4.4)/2.8 + (1.2 + 3.8 - 4.4)/5.2 = 0.071 + 0.115 = +0.19: fine-skewed (a tail of fine grains)."),
        PQ("Explain why a well-sorted, well-rounded quartz sand is described as supermature, and suggest an environment in which it may form.", "It has lost its clay matrix, its grains are of nearly one size and they are well rounded; only resistant quartz survives. This needs long reworking by waves or wind, for example on a beach or a desert dune field, or recycling of an older sandstone."),
        SUM("Wentworth scale: clay, silt, sand (1/16 to 2 mm), gravel; phi = -log2 d, so coarse grains are negative.",
            "Sieve data give a cumulative curve from which phi5, phi16, phi50, phi84 and phi95 are read.",
            "Folk and Ward: mean (phi16 + phi50 + phi84)/3; sorting (phi84 - phi16)/4 + (phi95 - phi5)/6.6; skewness from the same percentiles.",
            "Mean shows energy, sorting shows reworking, skewness shows winnowing or addition of fines.",
            "Statistics help to identify environments but overlap: combine them with structures and fossils."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Sedimentary structures
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Sedimentary structures",
    title="Sedimentary structures: bedforms, way-up and palaeocurrents",
    slug="gyu1-sedimentary-structures-way-up",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Classifying sedimentary structures as erosional, depositional, post-depositional and biogenic; linking ripples, dunes and plane beds to flow regime; using structures to find the way up of beds and the direction of ancient currents, including the vector mean.",
    objectives=[
        "Classify sedimentary structures by when and how they form",
        "Relate ripples, dunes, plane beds and antidunes to flow strength, and describe a turbidite (Bouma) sequence",
        "Use structures as way-up criteria in folded or overturned beds",
        "Find palaeocurrent directions from structures and calculate a vector mean",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Bedform", "Flow regime", "Cross-bedding", "Graded bedding", "Sole marks", "Load cast",
                  "Way-up criteria", "Palaeocurrent", "Vector mean", "Bouma sequence"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In a road cutting the beds may be tilted, folded or even upside down. How do you know which way was originally up, and which way the ancient river flowed? Sedimentary structures answer both questions. They are large-scale features of a bed, seen in the field or in a hand specimen, that record the processes acting during or just after deposition."),
        KEY("Four groups of structures", "Classify by when they formed.", cards=[
            ["Erosional (before the bed)", "Scours, channels, flute casts and tool marks cut into a mud surface and preserved as casts on the base (sole) of the overlying sand: **sole marks**."],
            ["Depositional (during)", "Bedding and lamination, ripple and dune cross-bedding, graded bedding, hummocky cross-stratification."],
            ["Post-depositional (soft-sediment)", "Load casts, flame structures, convolute bedding, slumps, dewatering pipes; desiccation cracks when mud dries out."],
            ["Biogenic", "Burrows, tracks and trails (trace fossils) and bioturbation, which can destroy lamination."]]),
        H("Bedforms and flow regime"),
        T("As a unidirectional current over sand gets stronger, the bed surface changes in a regular order. In the **lower flow regime**: no movement, then ripples (wavelength under about 60 cm, in sand finer than about 0.7 mm), then dunes (larger, called megaripples in older books). In the **upper flow regime**: a flat **plane bed** with parting lineation, then **antidunes** that migrate upstream. Migrating ripples and dunes build **cross-lamination** and **cross-bedding**."),
        D("**Cross-bedding** is inclined layering (foresets) inside a bed, formed as grains avalanche down the steep lee side of a migrating ripple or dune. The foresets dip **downstream**.", title="Cross-bedding"),
        IMG("algyu1-structures.svg", alt="Four panels: cross-bedding with foresets curving down to the right and tangential at the base, current arrow to the right; two graded beds coarse at the base and fine at the top; asymmetrical current ripples above symmetrical wave ripples; desiccation cracks narrowing downward into mud.", caption="Figure 1. Common sedimentary structures. All four can show way up."),
        KEY("Way-up (younging) criteria", "Each structure has a top and a bottom.", cards=[
            ["Cross-bedding", "Foresets are cut off sharply at the top and curve to become tangential to the base. An upside-down bed shows the curve meeting the top."],
            ["Graded bedding", "Grain size fines upward in each bed, from a sharp, often eroded base."],
            ["Desiccation cracks", "Cracks narrow downward and are filled from the bed above."],
            ["Sole marks and load casts", "Flutes, tool marks and load casts hang down from the base of a sand bed into the mud below; flame structures of mud point up."],
            ["Wave ripples", "Sharp crests point up and rounded troughs point down."]]),
        H("Turbidites: the Bouma sequence"),
        T("A **turbidity current** is a dense, sediment-laden flow that rushes down a submarine slope and then slows. Its deposit, a **turbidite**, often shows the **Bouma sequence** from base to top: Ta massive or graded sand; Tb parallel lamination; Tc ripple cross-lamination and convolute lamination; Td parallel-laminated silt; Te pelagic mud. The order records falling flow strength. Greywackes of deep-sea fans commonly show graded bedding, sole marks, flame structures and slumps."),
        H("Palaeocurrents"),
        T("Structures that give **direction**: cross-bed foresets and asymmetrical ripple lee sides (dip downstream), flute casts (the deep, blunt end points upstream) and the long axes of imbricated pebbles (dip upstream). Structures such as tool marks and parting lineation give only a **trend** (two possible directions). Many readings are plotted on a **rose diagram** and summarised by the **vector mean**."),
        F(r"\bar{\theta} = \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{\sum \sin\theta_i}{\sum \cos\theta_i}\right)", title="Vector mean of azimuths (choose the quadrant from the signs of the sums)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: why not the ordinary average?", intro="Two cross-bed foreset dip directions are 340 degrees and 020 degrees. Find the mean palaeocurrent.", steps=[
            S("Ordinary average", math=r"\frac{340 + 20}{2} = 180^{\circ}", text="This points south, which is clearly wrong: both readings point roughly north."),
            S("Sum the sines", math=r"\sin 340^{\circ} + \sin 20^{\circ} = -0.342 + 0.342 = 0"),
            S("Sum the cosines", math=r"\cos 340^{\circ} + \cos 20^{\circ} = 0.940 + 0.940 = 1.879"),
            S("Vector mean", text="Sine sum 0, cosine sum positive: the mean is 000 degrees (north)."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(0/1.879) = 0^{\circ}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: four readings", intro="Foreset dip directions: 350, 010, 030 and 020 degrees.", steps=[
            S("Sine sum", math=r"-0.174 + 0.174 + 0.500 + 0.342 = 0.842"),
            S("Cosine sum", math=r"0.985 + 0.985 + 0.866 + 0.940 = 3.775"),
            S("Angle", math=r"\tan^{-1}(0.842/3.775) = 12.6^{\circ}", text="Both sums are positive, so the angle is in the north-east quadrant."),
            S("Answer", text="The mean palaeocurrent is towards about 013 degrees (the ordinary average, 102.5 degrees, is wrong)."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(0.2231) = 12.6^{\circ}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: way up in a folded sequence", intro="On one limb of a fold, graded beds have their coarse grains at the top of each bed and cross-bed foresets meet the upper surface tangentially. What does this tell you?", steps=[
            S("Grading", text="Coarse grains at the top means the bed is upside down."),
            S("Cross-bedding", text="The tangential part should be at the base; here it is at the top, which agrees: inverted."),
            S("Structure", text="This limb is overturned, so the fold is an overturned fold. Two independent criteria agreeing makes the conclusion reliable."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Confusing flute casts and tool marks", "Flute casts are spoon-shaped, with a deep blunt end upstream, so they give direction. Tool marks (grooves and prods) are straight scratches made by objects dragged along, so they give only a trend."),
        MISTAKE("Averaging azimuths arithmetically", "Readings either side of north (for example 350 and 010 degrees) average to 180 degrees arithmetically, the opposite direction. Use the vector mean."),
        QUIZ("Cross-bed foresets in a sandstone dip towards 080 degrees. The current flowed", ["Towards about 080 degrees", "Towards about 260 degrees", "North-south", "Cannot be told"], 0, "Foresets dip downstream, so the current flowed towards their dip direction.", hint="Which way does the lee side of a dune face?", feedback=["", "That is the upstream direction.", "The strike of the foresets is not the flow direction.", "Cross-bedding gives a direction, not just a trend."]),
        QUIZ("Which structure gives only a trend, not a direction, of the current?", ["Flute casts", "Asymmetrical current ripples", "Groove (tool) marks", "Cross-bedding"], 2, "A straight groove can be followed both ways.", hint="Which mark has no asymmetry?", feedback=["Flutes are asymmetrical: the blunt end is upstream.", "The steep lee side faces downstream.", "", "Foresets dip downstream."]),
        TIP("Sedimentary structures appear in Paper 1 (identification and way up), in Paper 2 essays on environments (for example structures in deep marine greywackes or in transgressive shoreline sandstones) and in Paper 3 photographs and map work, where way up decides whether a fold limb is overturned.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe and explain the formation of graded bedding and state its use to a field geologist.", "Graded bedding is a bed in which grain size decreases from a coarse base to a fine top. It forms when a sediment-laden flow, such as a turbidity current, slows down: the largest grains settle first (Stokes' law), followed by finer grains. It is a way-up criterion: in an inverted bed the coarse grains are at the top."),
        PQ("Calculate the vector mean of the palaeocurrent readings 300, 330, 350, 010 and 020 degrees.", "Sine sum = -0.866 - 0.500 - 0.174 + 0.174 + 0.342 = -1.024. Cosine sum = 0.500 + 0.866 + 0.985 + 0.985 + 0.940 = 4.276. Angle = arctan(-1.024/4.276) = -13.5 degrees; sine negative and cosine positive means the north-west quadrant, so the mean is 346.5 degrees (about 347)."),
        PQ("Name the five divisions of the Bouma sequence and explain why the sequence fines upward.", "Ta massive or graded sand, Tb parallel-laminated sand, Tc ripple cross-laminated (and convolute) fine sand, Td parallel-laminated silt, Te mud. A turbidity current loses speed as it spreads out; coarse grains drop first, and the bedform changes from upper-flow-regime plane bed (Tb) to ripples (Tc) to settling from suspension (Td, Te)."),
        SUM("Structures are erosional, depositional, post-depositional or biogenic.",
            "With increasing flow: ripples, dunes (lower regime), then plane bed and antidunes (upper regime).",
            "Way-up: tangential bases of foresets, fining-up grading, downward-narrowing cracks, sole marks and load casts on bed bases.",
            "Foresets, ripple lee sides and flutes give direction; tool marks give only a trend.",
            "Average azimuths with the vector mean, never the arithmetic mean.",
            "Turbidites show the Bouma sequence Ta to Te."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Diagenesis
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Diagenesis",
    title="Diagenesis: compaction, cementation and porosity loss",
    slug="gyu1-diagenesis-compaction-cementation",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Diagenesis turns loose sediment into rock: compaction, pressure solution, cementation, dissolution, replacement and recrystallization. Calculate thickness loss on compaction and porosity at depth, and explain why diagenesis controls the quality of aquifers and petroleum reservoirs.",
    objectives=[
        "Define diagenesis and distinguish it from weathering and metamorphism",
        "Describe compaction, pressure solution, cementation, dissolution, replacement and recrystallization",
        "Calculate thickness change on compaction and porosity at depth using an exponential law",
        "Explain how diagenesis controls porosity and permeability of reservoir and aquifer rocks",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Diagenesis", "Lithification", "Compaction", "Pressure solution", "Stylolite", "Cementation",
                  "Secondary porosity", "Replacement", "Dolomitization", "Concretion", "Porosity"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Sand on a beach is loose; a sandstone is hard enough to build with. The change happens slowly as the sediment is buried under younger layers. Diagenesis matters well beyond the classroom: it decides how much water a sandstone aquifer can supply to a town, and how much oil a reservoir rock in a sedimentary basin can hold."),
        D("**Diagenesis** is the sum of all physical, chemical and biological changes that affect a sediment after deposition and during and after lithification, at the low temperatures and pressures of the upper few kilometres of the crust, before metamorphism begins. **Lithification** is the part of diagenesis that turns loose sediment into solid rock. The boundary with metamorphism (often put near 200 degrees C) is gradational; check your textbook.", title="Diagenesis and lithification"),
        D("**Porosity** is the percentage of a rock's volume that is pore space. **Permeability** is the ability of the rock to let fluids flow through it, which depends on how well the pores are connected.", title="Porosity and permeability", formula=r"\phi = \frac{V_{\text{pores}}}{V_{\text{total}}} \times 100\%"),
        IMG("algyu1-diagenesis.svg", alt="Three panels: loose rounded grains with large pores; the same grains packed closer and touching with interpenetrating contacts; the grains surrounded by thin rims and pores filled with cement. An arrow shows increasing burial.", caption="Figure 1. Diagenesis of a sand during burial (schematic)."),
        KEY("Diagenetic processes", "Learn each with its effect on porosity.", cards=[
            ["Mechanical compaction", "Grains are rearranged into closer packing and soft grains (mud clasts, micas) are squashed. Water is expelled. Muds lose most porosity this way: from 60 to 80 per cent at deposition to under 20 per cent."],
            ["Chemical compaction (pressure solution)", "At grain contacts the high stress dissolves minerals, giving concavo-convex and sutured contacts. On a larger scale it makes **stylolites**, jagged seams of insoluble residue, common in limestones. The dissolved silica or calcite feeds cement nearby."],
            ["Cementation", "Minerals precipitate from pore water: quartz overgrowths, calcite, haematite (red sandstones), clay minerals. Cement binds the grains and reduces porosity."],
            ["Dissolution", "Unstable grains (feldspar, shell fragments) or earlier cement dissolve, creating **secondary porosity**, which can improve a reservoir."],
            ["Replacement", "One mineral replaces another, volume for volume: dolomitization of limestone, silicification of shells (chert), pyritization of fossils in reducing mud."],
            ["Recrystallization", "A mineral changes crystal size or polymorph, for example aragonite shells to calcite, or fine micrite to coarser spar."]]),
        T("Diagenesis is often divided into **eogenesis** (near the surface, controlled by the depositional water), **mesogenesis** (during deep burial) and **telogenesis** (after uplift, when meteoric water enters). Concretions and nodules (for example of calcite, siderite, chert or pyrite) grow around a nucleus such as a fossil during early diagenesis, which is why they often preserve uncompacted fossils."),
        H("Calculating compaction"),
        T("During compaction the volume of solid grains stays the same; only pore space is lost. For a layer of unit area, solid thickness = h(1 - porosity) before and after, so:"),
        F(r"h_2 = h_1 \, \frac{1 - \phi_1}{1 - \phi_2}", title="Thickness after compaction (porosity as a fraction)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: compaction of a mud", intro="A 10 m layer of mud with porosity 0.60 is buried and compacted to a mudstone with porosity 0.20. Find its new thickness and the percentage reduction.", steps=[
            S("Solid thickness", math=r"10 \times (1 - 0.60) = 4.0\text{ m}"),
            S("New thickness", math=r"h_2 = \frac{4.0}{1 - 0.20} = \frac{4.0}{0.80} = 5.0\text{ m}"),
            S("Reduction", math=r"\frac{10 - 5}{10} \times 100 = 50\%"),
            S("Meaning", text="Mudstones have lost about half their thickness. A sandstone lens inside them, compacting less, will appear thicker relative to the mud, and fossils in mud are often flattened."),
        ], check=r"5.0 \times 0.80 = 4.0"),
        T("Porosity in many basins falls roughly exponentially with depth (Athy's relation). Here $\\phi_0$ is surface porosity, c a constant for the sediment and z the depth:"),
        F(r"\phi = \phi_0 \, e^{-cz}", title="Exponential porosity-depth relation"),
        EX("Worked example 2: porosity at depth", intro="A shale has surface porosity 0.60 and c = 0.0005 per metre (values invented for practice). Find its porosity at 2 000 m and the depth at which porosity has halved.", steps=[
            S("At 2 000 m", math=r"\phi = 0.60\, e^{-0.0005 \times 2000} = 0.60\, e^{-1} = 0.22"),
            S("Half the surface value", math=r"\frac{1}{2} = e^{-cz} \Rightarrow z = \frac{\ln 2}{c} = \frac{0.693}{0.0005}"),
            S("Answer", math=r"z \approx 1\,390\text{ m}"),
        ], check=r"0.6 e^{-1} = 0.2207"),
        EX("Worked example 3: porosity from measurements", intro="A dry sandstone core of bulk volume 50 cm3 has a mass of 106 g. Its grains are quartz (density 2.65 g/cm3). Find its porosity.", steps=[
            S("Grain volume", math=r"\frac{106}{2.65} = 40\text{ cm}^3"),
            S("Pore volume", math=r"50 - 40 = 10\text{ cm}^3"),
            S("Porosity", math=r"\frac{10}{50} \times 100 = 20\%"),
        ], check=r"106/2.65 = 40"),
        KEY("Why it matters", "Diagenesis decides reservoir and aquifer quality.", cards=[
            ["Good reservoirs", "Well-sorted, clean (matrix-free) sands with early grain-coating clays or early oil filling, which slow quartz cementation; or rocks with secondary porosity from dissolution."],
            ["Poor reservoirs", "Matrix-rich greywackes, heavily cemented sandstones, compacted mudstones."],
            ["Dolomitization", "Replacing calcite by dolomite, mole for mole, reduces the volume of solid by roughly an eighth, so it can increase porosity; this is one reason some dolomites are good reservoirs. Check your textbook for the details."]]),
        MISTAKE("Calling diagenesis weathering or metamorphism", "Weathering breaks rock down at the surface before transport. Diagenesis happens after deposition, mostly during burial. Metamorphism needs higher temperature and pressure and makes new metamorphic minerals and textures."),
        MISTAKE("Subtracting porosities to get the new thickness", "From 60 to 20 per cent porosity does not mean the bed loses 40 per cent of its thickness. Keep the solid volume constant: h2 = h1(1 - phi1)/(1 - phi2), which here gives a 50 per cent loss."),
        QUIZ("Jagged seams of dark insoluble residue in a limestone are called", ["Stylolites", "Concretions", "Flame structures", "Desiccation cracks"], 0, "Stylolites form by pressure solution, which dissolves calcite and leaves clay and organic residue.", hint="Think of chemical compaction.", feedback=["", "Concretions are rounded masses of cement.", "Flame structures are soft-sediment deformation at a sand-mud interface.", "Desiccation cracks form when mud dries at the surface."]),
        QUIZ("Which process can increase the porosity of a sandstone during burial?", ["Quartz overgrowth cementation", "Mechanical compaction", "Dissolution of feldspar grains", "Pressure solution at grain contacts"], 2, "Dissolving unstable grains creates secondary porosity.", hint="Which process removes solid material from the rock?", feedback=["Cement fills pores and lowers porosity.", "Compaction squeezes out pore space.", "", "Pressure solution reduces volume and feeds cement."]),
        TIP("Diagenesis appears in Paper 1 (processes and terms) and Paper 2 essays on the formation of sedimentary rocks and of petroleum reservoirs. In Paper 3, the cement of a hand specimen (for example haematite in a red sandstone, calcite that fizzes with dilute acid) is part of a full description.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain how a loose quartz sand becomes a quartz-cemented sandstone.", "During burial, overburden pressure causes mechanical compaction (closer packing, expulsion of water). At greater depth and temperature, pressure solution dissolves quartz where grains touch. The dissolved silica moves in pore water and precipitates as quartz overgrowths in optical continuity on grains in pores nearby. Cement binds the grains, porosity falls and the sand is lithified."),
        PQ("A 20 m layer of sand with porosity 0.50 is compacted to porosity 0.10. Calculate its new thickness.", "Solid thickness = 20 x 0.50 = 10 m. New thickness = 10 / 0.90 = 11.1 m."),
        PQ("Essay plan: 'Diagenesis always destroys porosity.' Discuss.", "Plan: (1) Define diagenesis and porosity. (2) Processes that destroy porosity: mechanical compaction, pressure solution, cementation by quartz, calcite, haematite and clays. (3) Processes that create or preserve porosity: dissolution of feldspar, shells or earlier cement (secondary porosity); dolomitization; early grain-coating clays or early oil filling that stop quartz overgrowths. (4) Evaluate: in most basins porosity falls with depth (exponential law), so the statement is broadly true, but many important reservoirs owe their quality to secondary porosity. (5) Conclusion: the net effect depends on mineralogy, burial history and pore fluids."),
        SUM("Diagenesis: changes after deposition at low temperature and pressure; lithification turns sediment into rock.",
            "Compaction (mechanical and chemical, with stylolites) and cementation reduce porosity.",
            "Dissolution creates secondary porosity; replacement and recrystallization change mineralogy.",
            "Solid volume is conserved: h2 = h1(1 - phi1)/(1 - phi2).",
            "Porosity often falls exponentially with depth: phi = phi0 e^(-cz).",
            "Diagenesis controls the quality of aquifers and petroleum reservoirs."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Siliciclastic rocks
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Siliciclastic rocks",
    title="Siliciclastic rocks: conglomerates, sandstones and mudrocks",
    slug="gyu1-siliciclastic-rocks-classification",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Classify and describe conglomerates, breccias, sandstones and mudrocks: QFL point counts, arenites and wackes, arkose, greywacke and quartz arenite, maturity, and the rocks typical of piedmont, deep-marine and transgressive shoreline settings.",
    objectives=[
        "Classify clastic rocks by grain size and conglomerates by clast type and fabric",
        "Recalculate point-count data to QFL percentages and name a sandstone",
        "Describe composition, texture, maturity and structures of arkose, greywacke, quartz arenite and black shale",
        "Relate siliciclastic rocks to their source area and depositional environment",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Siliciclastic", "Conglomerate", "Breccia", "Oligomict", "Polymict", "Arenite", "Wacke", "Arkose",
                  "Greywacke", "Quartz arenite", "Mineralogical maturity", "Shale"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("**Siliciclastic** rocks are made of fragments of silicate minerals and rocks produced by weathering and erosion. Their grains are a record of the source area: a sandstone full of pink feldspar points to nearby granite, like the granites and gneisses of Cameroon's basement, eroded quickly in a hot or dry climate. This lesson builds the descriptions you need for essays and for hand specimens in the practical paper."),
        KEY("Grain size first", "The main division uses the Wentworth classes.", cards=[
            ["Rudites (over 2 mm)", "Conglomerate (rounded clasts) and breccia (angular clasts)."],
            ["Arenites in the wide sense (1/16 to 2 mm)", "Sandstones."],
            ["Lutites (under 1/16 mm)", "Siltstone, mudstone (blocky) and shale (fissile: splits into thin sheets)."]]),
        KEY("Describing conglomerates", "Name the clasts, the fabric and the shape.", cards=[
            ["Oligomict", "Clasts of one resistant type, usually quartz, chert or quartzite, well rounded and well sorted: long reworking, for example the lag at the base of a transgressing sea."],
            ["Polymict", "Clasts of many rock types, poorly sorted: short transport from a varied source, for example alluvial fans in a piedmont."],
            ["Clast-supported", "Clasts touch each other; the spaces hold sand or cement (stream and beach gravels)."],
            ["Matrix-supported", "Clasts float in mud or sand (debris flows, glacial till). A poorly sorted, matrix-supported rock of any origin is a diamictite."],
            ["Breccia", "Angular clasts, little transport: scree, fault breccia, collapse breccia."]]),
        H("Classifying sandstones"),
        T("Under the microscope a geologist counts a few hundred points and records what lies under each: quartz (Q, including chert), feldspar (F), rock fragments (L, for lithic), and fine-grained **matrix** (clay and silt under about 0.03 mm). In the widely used scheme based on Pettijohn and Dott, a sandstone with **less than 15 per cent matrix** is an **arenite** and one with more is a **wacke**. Arenites are then named from Q, F and L recalculated to 100 per cent. Textbooks differ in the limits; check your textbook."),
        IMG("algyu1-qfl.svg", alt="QFL triangle with Q at the top, F bottom left and L bottom right. Lines at 95 and 75 per cent quartz and a vertical line where F equals L divide quartz arenite, subarkose, sublitharenite, arkose and lithic arenite. Point A lies in the arkose field.", caption="Figure 1. Classification of arenites (simplified). Point A is Worked example 1."),
        EX("Worked example 1: naming a sandstone", intro="A point count of 200 points gives quartz 120, feldspar 54, rock fragments 18 and matrix 8. Name the rock.", steps=[
            S("Matrix percentage", math=r"\frac{8}{200} \times 100 = 4\%", text="Under 15 per cent: an arenite."),
            S("Framework total", math=r"120 + 54 + 18 = 192"),
            S("Recalculate", math=r"Q = \frac{120}{192} = 62.5\%,\quad F = 28.1\%,\quad L = 9.4\%"),
            S("Plot and name", text="Q is below 75 per cent and F is greater than L: an **arkose** (point A in Figure 1)."),
        ], check=r"62.5 + 28.1 + 9.4 = 100"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a wacke", intro="A count gives quartz 100, feldspar 20, rock fragments 40 and matrix 40, out of 200 points. Name the rock.", steps=[
            S("Matrix", math=r"\frac{40}{200} \times 100 = 20\%", text="Over 15 per cent: a wacke (greywacke in the traditional sense)."),
            S("Framework", math=r"Q = \frac{100}{160} = 62.5\%,\ F = 12.5\%,\ L = 25\%"),
            S("Name", text="L exceeds F, so it is a lithic wacke (lithic greywacke)."),
        ], check=r"100 + 20 + 40 = 160"),
        H("Maturity and three important sandstones"),
        T("**Mineralogical maturity** measures how far unstable grains (feldspar, rock fragments, ferromagnesian minerals) have been destroyed, leaving quartz. A simple index is Q/(F + L). **Textural maturity** (Lesson 2) measures matrix, sorting and rounding. A long, slow journey in a warm, wet climate gives mature rocks; rapid erosion and burial gives immature ones."),
        KEY("Typical siliciclastic rocks and their settings", "A model description gives composition, texture (grain size, shape, sorting, maturity) and structures.", cards=[
            ["Arkose (piedmont, desert basin)", "Quartz and more than about 25 per cent feldspar, some mica and rock fragments; medium to coarse grained, angular to subrounded, moderately to poorly sorted; submature; cross-bedding. Fast erosion of granite or gneiss with little chemical weathering."],
            ["Red (ferruginous) sandstone (desert)", "Quartz with haematite coatings and cement; medium grained, well rounded, well sorted, mature to supermature; large-scale aeolian cross-bedding."],
            ["Greywacke (deep marine)", "Quartz, feldspar and many rock fragments in over 15 per cent clayey matrix; poorly sorted, angular; immature. Graded bedding, sole marks, flame structures, slumps: turbidity currents."],
            ["Black shale (deep, low-oxygen water)", "Clay minerals, quartz silt, organic matter and pyrite; fine grained, laminated. Organic matter survives because there is little oxygen."],
            ["Quartz arenite or orthoquartzite (transgressive shoreline)", "Over 95 per cent quartz, silica cement; medium grained, well rounded, well sorted, supermature; cross-bedding, ripple marks. Often above a basal oligomict conglomerate."]]),
        MISTAKE("Naming a mineral as a rock", "In an essay on piedmont rocks, 'muscovite' or 'feldspar' is not an answer: name the rock (arkose, breccia, polymict conglomerate) and then describe its minerals. Examiners reported this confusion."),
        MISTAKE("Using igneous textures for sedimentary rocks", "Words such as porphyritic or phaneritic do not describe sandstones. Use grain size (coarse grained, medium grained), grain shape, sorting and maturity, and name the cement and matrix."),
        MISTAKE("Calculating QFL including the matrix", "Matrix decides arenite or wacke. Recalculate Q, F and L to 100 per cent of the framework grains only."),
        QUIZ("A sandstone has 22 per cent clay matrix, angular grains of quartz, feldspar and rock fragments, and graded bedding. It is most likely", ["A quartz arenite from a beach", "A greywacke from a turbidite", "An aeolian red sandstone", "An oligomict conglomerate"], 1, "Over 15 per cent matrix with angular, mixed grains and grading points to a greywacke deposited by turbidity currents.", hint="What does more than 15 per cent matrix make the rock?", feedback=["Quartz arenites have almost no matrix and are well rounded.", "", "Aeolian sandstones are matrix-free and well sorted.", "A conglomerate has clasts over 2 mm."]),
        QUIZ("Which rock is mineralogically the most mature?", ["Arkose", "Greywacke", "Quartz arenite", "Polymict conglomerate"], 2, "Quartz arenite is over 95 per cent quartz: unstable grains have been removed.", hint="Maturity rises as unstable grains are destroyed.", feedback=["Arkose keeps much feldspar: immature to submature.", "Greywacke has many unstable fragments and matrix.", "", "Polymict clasts include unstable rock types."]),
        TIP("The Board's report shows Paper 2 essays asking for the rocks of named environments with their composition, texture and structures, and Paper 3 hand specimens such as red sandstone. Learn one complete model description per environment and always write 'coarse grained', 'well sorted', 'mature'.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("A point count gives Q 160, F 10, L 30 and matrix 10. Name the sandstone, showing your working.", "Matrix = 10/210 = 4.8 per cent, so it is an arenite. Framework total = 200: Q = 80 per cent, F = 5 per cent, L = 15 per cent. Q lies between 75 and 95 per cent and L exceeds F: a sublitharenite."),
        PQ("Compare an oligomict and a polymict conglomerate in terms of composition, texture and environment.", "Oligomict: clasts of one resistant type (vein quartz, chert, quartzite), well rounded and well sorted, supermature, often with silica cement; formed by long reworking, for example the basal conglomerate of a transgressing sea. Polymict: clasts of many rock types including unstable ones, subangular to subrounded, poorly sorted, often matrix-rich and immature; formed by rapid deposition near a varied source, for example alluvial fans in a piedmont."),
        PQ("Essay plan: describe a rock typical of a deep marine environment and account for its features.", "Plan: (1) Name: greywacke (or black shale). (2) Composition: quartz, feldspar, rock fragments, over 15 per cent clayey matrix. (3) Texture: fine to coarse grained, angular to subangular, poorly sorted, immature. (4) Structures: graded bedding, sole marks, flame structures, slumps, laminations (Bouma sequence). (5) Explanation: sediment is carried down a submarine slope by turbidity currents and dumped quickly as the flow slows, so there is no time for sorting or rounding and mud is trapped between grains; quiet periods deposit pelagic mud between turbidites."),
        SUM("Siliciclastic rocks are classified first by grain size: rudites, sandstones, mudrocks.",
            "Conglomerates: oligomict or polymict, clast- or matrix-supported; breccias have angular clasts.",
            "Sandstones: arenite under 15 per cent matrix, wacke over; name arenites from QFL recalculated to 100 per cent.",
            "Arkose (feldspar-rich), greywacke (matrix-rich, turbidites), quartz arenite (supermature, shorelines and dunes).",
            "Always describe composition, texture (grain size, shape, sorting, maturity) and structures, then link to environment."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Carbonate rocks
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Carbonate rocks",
    title="Carbonate rocks: components and the Folk and Dunham classifications",
    slug="gyu1-carbonate-rocks-folk-dunham",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Limestones are built by organisms in warm, clear, shallow seas. Learn their components (allochems, micrite, sparite), name them with the Folk and Dunham schemes, link them to environments from lagoon to reef to deep sea, and find carbonate content by acid digestion.",
    objectives=[
        "Identify the components of limestones: allochems, micrite and sparite",
        "Name limestones using the Folk and the Dunham classifications",
        "Relate carbonate rock types to depositional environment and the conditions of carbonate production",
        "Distinguish limestone from dolomite and calculate carbonate content from acid digestion data",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Allochem", "Ooid", "Bioclast", "Peloid", "Intraclast", "Micrite", "Sparite", "Folk classification",
                  "Dunham classification", "Dolomite", "Carbonate compensation depth"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Limestone is quarried in Cameroon for cement and lime; the limestone of the Figuil area in the North Region is a well-known example (check your textbook for local details). Unlike sandstones, most limestones are not transported from far away: they are made where they are found, largely by organisms that take calcium carbonate from sea water. To read a limestone you must identify its grains, its mud and its cement."),
        KEY("Where carbonate forms: the carbonate factory", "Most carbonate is produced in", cards=[
            ["Warm water", "Carbonate is less soluble in warm water, and corals and green algae thrive between about 30 degrees north and south."],
            ["Shallow, clear, sunlit water", "Photosynthesising algae and the algae living in corals need light; mud and sand from rivers would smother them."],
            ["Little clastic input", "Where rivers bring much sand and mud, carbonate is diluted."],
            ["Deep sea", "Plankton (coccoliths, foraminifera) form chalk and carbonate ooze, but only above the **carbonate compensation depth** (CCD), below which carbonate dissolves."]]),
        H("Components of limestones"),
        KEY("Folk's three components", "Every limestone is a mixture of these.", cards=[
            ["Allochems (grains)", "Ooids (spheres with concentric layers, formed by rolling in agitated, warm, supersaturated shallow water); bioclasts (shell fragments, whole fossils); peloids (small ovoid grains of micrite, often faecal pellets); intraclasts (reworked fragments of partly lithified carbonate sediment)."],
            ["Micrite", "Microcrystalline carbonate mud, crystals under about 4 micrometres; dull and opaque-looking. It settles in quiet water."],
            ["Sparite", "Clear, coarse calcite crystals that grew as a cement in open pores. It shows that the water was energetic enough to wash the mud out."]]),
        D("In **Folk's classification** the name has two parts: a prefix for the main allochem (oo-, bio-, pel-, intra-) and a root for what lies between the grains: **-sparite** (cement) or **-micrite** (mud). For example **oosparite**, **biomicrite**, **pelmicrite**. A rock with almost no allochems is a micrite; a reef rock built in place is a **biolithite**.", title="Folk classification (1959, 1962)"),
        D("**Dunham's classification** looks at the depositional texture: whether there is mud, and whether the grains or the mud support the rock. Mud-supported with under 10 per cent grains: **mudstone**; with over 10 per cent grains: **wackestone**. Grain-supported with mud between: **packstone**; without mud: **grainstone**. Components bound together during deposition: **boundstone**. If the original texture is destroyed by recrystallization: crystalline carbonate.", title="Dunham classification (1962)"),
        IMG("algyu1-dunham.svg", alt="Chart of five sketched thin sections: mudstone with a few grains in mud, wackestone with more grains floating in mud, packstone with touching grains and mud between, grainstone with touching grains and clear cement, and boundstone with layered organic framework.", caption="Figure 1. Dunham's classification. Embry and Klovan later added floatstone and rudstone for coarse grains; check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: naming in both schemes", intro="A thin section shows 60 per cent ooids, all touching each other, with clear coarse calcite crystals filling the spaces and no mud. Name the rock and interpret it.", steps=[
            S("Folk", text="Main allochem: ooids (oo-). Between grains: sparite. Name: **oosparite**."),
            S("Dunham", text="Grain-supported, no mud: **grainstone** (oolitic grainstone)."),
            S("Interpretation", text="Ooids form in agitated, warm, shallow water such as tidal shoals; the lack of mud confirms constant wave or tidal action that winnowed it away."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a lagoon limestone", intro="A dark, fine limestone has 20 per cent scattered whole shells floating in carbonate mud. Name it and interpret it.", steps=[
            S("Folk", text="Bioclasts in micrite: **biomicrite**."),
            S("Dunham", text="Mud-supported with over 10 per cent grains: **wackestone**."),
            S("Interpretation", text="Quiet, protected water where mud settles and shelled animals lived: a lagoon or a deeper shelf below wave base."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: carbonate content", intro="A 5.00 g limestone sample is dissolved in excess dilute hydrochloric acid. The insoluble residue (clay and quartz) weighs 0.40 g. Find the carbonate content and the mass of carbon dioxide released, assuming the carbonate is all calcite.", steps=[
            S("Carbonate mass", math=r"5.00 - 0.40 = 4.60\text{ g}"),
            S("Percentage", math=r"\frac{4.60}{5.00} \times 100 = 92\%"),
            S("Reaction", math=r"\mathrm{CaCO_3 + 2HCl \rightarrow CaCl_2 + H_2O + CO_2}"),
            S("Carbon dioxide", math=r"4.60 \times \frac{44}{100} = 2.02\text{ g}"),
        ], check=r"4.60/5.00 = 0.92"),
        KEY("Limestone or dolomite?", "Dolostone (dolomite rock) is CaMg(CO3)2, usually formed by replacement of limestone.", cards=[
            ["Acid test", "Calcite fizzes strongly in cold dilute hydrochloric acid. Dolomite fizzes only weakly, when powdered or in warm acid."],
            ["Stain", "Alizarin red S stains calcite red but leaves dolomite unstained."],
            ["Appearance", "Dolomites are often sugary (crystalline), buff-weathering, with rhomb-shaped crystals and moulds of dissolved fossils."]]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up micrite and sparite", "Micrite is mud deposited in quiet water. Sparite is a cement grown later in pore space. Sparite between grains means the mud was washed out; it is not a sign of quiet water."),
        MISTAKE("Calling the Dunham 'mudstone' a clastic mudstone", "In Dunham's scheme a mudstone is a carbonate mudstone (lime mudstone). Write 'lime mudstone' or 'carbonate mudstone' to avoid confusion with siliciclastic mudrocks."),
        QUIZ("A limestone consists of touching shell fragments with carbonate mud filling the spaces between them. Its Dunham name is", ["Wackestone", "Packstone", "Grainstone", "Boundstone"], 1, "Grain-supported with mud between the grains is a packstone.", hint="Do the grains touch, and is there mud?", feedback=["In a wackestone the grains float in mud.", "", "A grainstone has no mud.", "Boundstone needs organisms binding the sediment during deposition."]),
        QUIZ("Which Folk name suits a rock of peloids in a matrix of carbonate mud?", ["Oosparite", "Pelmicrite", "Biosparite", "Intrasparite"], 1, "Pel- for peloids, -micrite for mud.", hint="Name the grain, then what lies between.", feedback=["Oo- means ooids and -sparite means cement.", "", "Bio- means fossils; -sparite means cement.", "Intra- means intraclasts."]),
        TIP("Limestone classification and environments suit Paper 1 and Paper 2 essays on carbonate rocks. In Paper 3 the acid test identifies limestone and marble (both contain calcite); the Board's report notes that candidates confused marble with calcite and quartz, so give the texture (granular, sugary) as well as the reaction with dilute HCl.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe the conditions needed for carbonate production on a shallow shelf.", "Warm, tropical water (carbonate is less soluble and reef organisms thrive); clear water with little clastic input from rivers; shallow, sunlit water for photosynthesis by algae and coral symbionts; normal marine salinity and good circulation supplying nutrients and carbonate."),
        PQ("Loss of carbon dioxide on dissolving a 5.00 g limestone sample in acid is 1.98 g. Assuming all carbonate is calcite, calculate the percentage of calcite.", "Mass of CaCO3 = 1.98 x 100/44 = 4.50 g. Percentage = 4.50/5.00 x 100 = 90 per cent."),
        PQ("Compare an oosparite and a biomicrite as indicators of environment.", "Oosparite (oolitic grainstone): ooids need constant agitation in warm, shallow, carbonate-saturated water; sparite cement shows mud was winnowed: a high-energy shoal or tidal bar. Biomicrite (wackestone or mudstone with fossils): mud settled in quiet water; the fossils lived there: a low-energy lagoon or a shelf below wave base. Both are shallow marine but differ in energy."),
        SUM("Carbonate forms mainly in warm, shallow, clear water with little clastic input; deep-sea ooze only above the CCD.",
            "Components: allochems (ooids, bioclasts, peloids, intraclasts), micrite (mud) and sparite (cement).",
            "Folk: allochem prefix plus micrite or sparite, for example oosparite, biomicrite.",
            "Dunham: mudstone, wackestone, packstone, grainstone, boundstone, by mud and grain support.",
            "Dolomite fizzes weakly in cold dilute acid and does not stain with alizarin red S.",
            "Carbonate content = (sample - insoluble residue)/sample x 100."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Evaporites and carbonaceous rocks
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Evaporites and carbonaceous rocks",
    title="Evaporites, coal and organic-rich rocks",
    slug="gyu1-evaporites-coal-carbonaceous-rocks",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Evaporites crystallize from brines in a set order (carbonate, gypsum, halite, potash salts) in sabkhas, salt lakes and barred basins. Coal forms from buried plant matter by coalification; oil shales and black shales store kerogen.",
    objectives=[
        "State the order of precipitation of evaporite minerals from sea water and explain it",
        "Describe sabkha, salt-lake and barred-basin models and calculate the salt from a column of sea water",
        "Explain the formation and rank series of coal and calculate compaction of peat to coal",
        "Distinguish humic coal, sapropelic coal, oil shale and black shale and state their economic importance",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Evaporite", "Gypsum", "Anhydrite", "Halite", "Sabkha", "Barred basin", "Salt dome", "Peat",
                  "Coalification", "Coal rank", "Kerogen", "Oil shale"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When sea water evaporates in a hot, dry climate the salts it carries crystallize as **evaporites**. When plant remains pile up in a waterlogged swamp faster than they rot, they become peat and, after burial, coal. Both groups are chemical or organic sedimentary rocks rather than clastic ones, and both are economically important: salt and gypsum for industry and building, coal for energy, and salt domes as traps for petroleum."),
        H("Evaporites"),
        D("An **evaporite** is a rock made of minerals precipitated from a brine concentrated by evaporation. Sea water holds about 35 g of dissolved salts per litre, mostly sodium chloride, with sulphate, magnesium, calcium and potassium.", title="Evaporite"),
        KEY("Order of precipitation from sea water", "Least soluble first. Fractions are approximate; check your textbook.", cards=[
            ["1. Calcium carbonate", "Calcite or aragonite once about half the water has evaporated."],
            ["2. Gypsum (CaSO4.2H2O) or anhydrite (CaSO4)", "When the volume is reduced to about one fifth (about 80 per cent evaporated)."],
            ["3. Halite (NaCl)", "When the volume is about one tenth of the original (about 90 per cent evaporated). Halite is most of the salt."],
            ["4. Potassium and magnesium salts", "Sylvite (KCl), carnallite and others, only from the last, very concentrated bitterns."]]),
        T("The order follows solubility: the least soluble salt reaches saturation first. Gypsum buried below about a kilometre loses its water and turns to anhydrite; near the surface anhydrite can take up water and swell back to gypsum, which damages foundations and tunnels."),
        EX("Worked example 1: how much salt does sea water hold?", intro="A column of sea water 1 000 m deep and 1 m2 in area evaporates completely. Taking 35 kg of salt per m3 and a density of 2 200 kg/m3 for the salt, find the thickness of evaporite formed.", steps=[
            S("Volume of water", math=r"1000 \times 1 = 1000\text{ m}^3"),
            S("Mass of salt", math=r"1000 \times 35 = 35\,000\text{ kg}"),
            S("Volume of salt", math=r"\frac{35\,000}{2200} = 15.9\text{ m}^3"),
            S("Thickness", text="About 16 m of evaporite over 1 m2, most of it halite."),
            S("Meaning", text="Evaporite sequences hundreds of metres thick cannot come from one basin-full of water: the basin must have been refilled again and again."),
        ], check=r"35000/2200 = 15.9"),
        KEY("Where evaporites form", "Three models.", cards=[
            ["Sabkha (coastal salt flat)", "On hot, arid coasts just above high tide. Gypsum and anhydrite grow inside the sediment as nodules ('chicken-wire' anhydrite) and the area is covered by microbial mats. Thin, repeated cycles."],
            ["Salt lakes and playas", "Inland basins with no outlet; seasonal flooding and drying give layered salts, often with unusual minerals such as trona."],
            ["Barred basin", "A basin cut off from the open sea by a sill. Sea water flows in over the sill and evaporates; brine becomes more concentrated away from the sill, so carbonate, gypsum, halite and potash salts are laid down in zones. Repeated inflow builds great thicknesses."]]),
        IMG("algyu1-evaporite.svg", alt="Cross-section of a barred basin: open sea on the left, a sill, then a basin with evaporation arrows above. Inflow passes over the sill. Zones on the basin floor away from the sill are 1 carbonate, 2 gypsum or anhydrite, 3 halite, 4 potash salts.", caption="Figure 1. A barred basin. In map view the zones form a 'teardrop' away from the inlet; a basin that dries from the edges inwards gives a 'bull's-eye' with the most soluble salts in the centre."),
        T("Halite is weak and less dense than most buried sediment, so thick salt can flow upward as **salt domes (diapirs)**, arching and piercing the beds above. These are among the major structures associated with folds that examiners expect, and they trap oil and gas."),
        H("Carbonaceous rocks"),
        D("**Coal** is a combustible rock formed from compacted plant remains. **Peat** accumulates in waterlogged, oxygen-poor mires where plants grow fast and decay is slow. Burial raises temperature and pressure; water and volatile matter are driven off and carbon is concentrated: **coalification**.", title="Coal and coalification"),
        KEY("Coal rank series", "Rank increases with the maximum burial temperature (and time). Carbon values are approximate, on a dry, ash-free basis; textbooks differ.", cards=[
            ["Peat", "Brown, fibrous plant debris; about 60 per cent carbon; very high moisture."],
            ["Lignite (brown coal)", "Soft, brown, plant structures visible; about 70 per cent carbon."],
            ["Sub-bituminous and bituminous coal", "Black, banded, bright and dull layers; about 75 to 90 per cent carbon; the main coking and power coals."],
            ["Anthracite", "Hard, black, glassy lustre, conchoidal fracture; over about 90 per cent carbon; low volatiles."]]),
        EX("Worked example 2: peat to coal", intro="Peat compacts to bituminous coal in a ratio of about 10 to 1 (textbook values range from about 5:1 to 20:1). How much peat formed a 1.5 m coal seam, and what does this suggest about the swamp?", steps=[
            S("Peat thickness", math=r"1.5 \times 10 = 15\text{ m}"),
            S("Interpretation", text="The mire must have stayed waterlogged, with subsidence keeping pace with plant growth, for a long time, and with little sand or mud washed in (otherwise the coal would be shaly)."),
        ], check=r"1.5 \times 10 = 15"),
        KEY("Other organic-rich rocks", "Kerogen is the insoluble organic matter in sedimentary rocks.", cards=[
            ["Humic coal", "From woody land plants: the normal banded coals."],
            ["Sapropelic coal (cannel and boghead)", "From algae, spores and fine plant debris settling in quiet lakes; dull, non-banded, rich in volatiles."],
            ["Oil shale", "Fine-grained rock rich in kerogen that yields oil when heated; formed in lakes or quiet seas with algal blooms."],
            ["Black shale", "Clay with organic matter and pyrite deposited in oxygen-poor (anoxic) water; an important petroleum source rock."]]),
        MISTAKE("Getting the precipitation order backwards", "Halite is very soluble, so it crystallizes after gypsum, not before. Least soluble first: carbonate, then gypsum or anhydrite, then halite, then potash salts."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking one sea-filling makes thick salt", "A kilometre of sea water gives only about 16 m of salt. Thick evaporites need repeated inflow, as in a barred basin, or subsidence with continued supply."),
        QUIZ("Which mineral crystallizes from evaporating sea water after gypsum but before potash salts?", ["Calcite", "Halite", "Sylvite", "Quartz"], 1, "Halite follows gypsum and precedes the potash and magnesium salts.", hint="Order by solubility.", feedback=["Calcite crystallizes before gypsum.", "", "Sylvite is a potash salt, among the last.", "Quartz is not an evaporite mineral."]),
        QUIZ("As coal rank increases from lignite to anthracite,", ["Carbon content falls", "Moisture and volatile matter fall and carbon content rises", "Plant structures become easier to see", "The coal becomes softer"], 1, "Coalification drives off water and volatiles and concentrates carbon.", hint="What does heating do to wet organic matter?", feedback=["Carbon content rises with rank.", "", "Plant structures are destroyed as rank rises.", "Anthracite is the hardest coal."]),
        TIP("Evaporites, salt domes and coal suit Paper 1 and Paper 2 essays on sedimentary rocks and on economic geology (salt domes as petroleum traps). Name minerals with their formulae where you can, and give the environment and the evidence for it.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain why evaporite minerals in a barred basin are arranged in zones away from the sill.", "Sea water enters over the sill and evaporates as it flows into the basin. Its salinity rises with distance from the sill. The least soluble minerals (carbonate, then gypsum) saturate first near the inlet; halite saturates further in; potash and magnesium salts only in the most concentrated brine farthest from the sill. In map view this gives a 'teardrop' pattern."),
        PQ("A halite sequence is 300 m thick. Using about 16 m of salt per 1 000 m of sea water, estimate the depth of sea water evaporated and comment.", "300/16 x 1 000 = about 19 000 m of sea water (19 km). No basin is that deep, so the basin must have been refilled many times while it subsided or filled, as in a barred basin with continuous inflow."),
        PQ("Describe the conditions needed for a thick coal seam to form.", "A waterlogged, oxygen-poor mire (swamp) where plants grow fast and decay slowly; a warm, humid climate helps plant growth; steady subsidence balancing peat accumulation for a long time; little input of sand and mud; then burial under later sediments, which supplies the heat and pressure for coalification."),
        SUM("Evaporites precipitate in order of solubility: carbonate, gypsum or anhydrite, halite, potash salts.",
            "Settings: sabkha (nodular anhydrite), salt lakes and barred basins (zones away from the sill).",
            "1 000 m of sea water gives only about 16 m of salt: thick evaporites need repeated refilling.",
            "Halite flows into salt domes, which trap petroleum.",
            "Coal: peat in waterlogged mires, then coalification; rank lignite, bituminous, anthracite with rising carbon.",
            "Oil shale and black shale store kerogen; black shales are major petroleum source rocks."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Stress and strain
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Stress and strain",
    title="Stress and strain: principal stresses, the Mohr circle and strain ellipses",
    slug="gyu1-stress-strain-mohr-circle",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Stress is force per area; in rocks it is described by three principal stresses and the stress ellipsoid. The Mohr circle gives normal and shear stress on any plane. Strain is the change in shape and size, measured by extension, shear strain and the strain ellipse.",
    objectives=[
        "Define normal and shear stress, principal stresses, lithostatic and differential stress",
        "Calculate the normal and shear stress on a plane with the formulae and the Mohr circle",
        "Calculate extension, stretch and shear strain, and describe the strain ellipse and ellipsoid",
        "Use deformed objects such as ooids or reduction spots to measure strain",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Stress", "Normal stress", "Shear stress", "Principal stresses", "Stress ellipsoid", "Lithostatic stress",
                  "Differential stress", "Mohr circle", "Extension", "Shear strain", "Strain ellipse", "Pure shear", "Simple shear"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("At O Level you learned that stress is force on a rock and strain is the change it causes. At A Level you must put numbers on both. Engineers designing a tunnel through the Cameroon highlands, and geologists explaining why a fault formed at a particular angle, use the same tools: principal stresses, the Mohr circle and the strain ellipse."),
        D("**Stress** is force per unit area, in pascals (1 MPa = 1 000 000 Pa). On any plane it has two parts: the **normal stress** (sigma_n) acting at right angles to the plane and the **shear stress** (tau) acting along it. Geologists usually count compression as positive.", title="Stress on a plane", formula=r"\sigma = \frac{F}{A}"),
        D("At any point there are three perpendicular planes with no shear stress on them. The stresses on these planes are the **principal stresses** sigma1 (greatest), sigma2 (intermediate) and sigma3 (least). They are the axes of the **stress ellipsoid**.", title="Principal stresses"),
        KEY("Kinds of stress state", "Learn the words used in essays.", cards=[
            ["Lithostatic stress", "Stress from the weight of overlying rock, equal in all directions: sigma = rho g h. It changes volume, not shape."],
            ["Differential stress", "sigma1 - sigma3. Only differential stress changes the shape of rock (folds, faults)."],
            ["Mean stress", "(sigma1 + sigma2 + sigma3)/3, the pressure part of the stress."],
            ["Tectonic setting", "Compression: sigma1 horizontal. Extension: sigma1 vertical (the weight of the rock) and sigma3 horizontal."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: lithostatic stress", intro="Find the lithostatic stress at a depth of 3 km under rock of density 2 700 kg/m3 (g = 9.81 m/s2).", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"\sigma = \rho g h"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"\sigma = 2700 \times 9.81 \times 3000"),
            S("Answer", math=r"\sigma = 7.95\times10^{7}\text{ Pa} \approx 79.5\text{ MPa}"),
        ], check=r"2700 \times 9.81 \times 3000 = 79\,461\,000"),
        H("Stress on a plane and the Mohr circle"),
        T("For a plane whose normal makes an angle theta with sigma1 (working in the sigma1-sigma3 plane):"),
        F(r"\sigma_n = \frac{\sigma_1 + \sigma_3}{2} + \frac{\sigma_1 - \sigma_3}{2}\cos 2\theta \qquad \tau = \frac{\sigma_1 - \sigma_3}{2}\sin 2\theta", title="Normal and shear stress on a plane"),
        T("These are the equations of a circle, the **Mohr circle**: centre at (sigma1 + sigma3)/2 on the normal stress axis and radius (sigma1 - sigma3)/2. A plane is a point on the circle at an angle 2 theta from the sigma1 end. Some textbooks measure theta from the plane itself rather than its normal, which changes the sign of the cosine term; check your textbook and always state your convention."),
        IMG("algyu1-mohr.svg", alt="Mohr circle (upper half) with sigma3 = 40 and sigma1 = 100 MPa on the normal stress axis, centre 70, radius 30. A radius at 2 theta = 60 degrees reaches a point with normal stress 85 and shear stress 26.", caption="Figure 1. Mohr circle for sigma1 = 100 MPa and sigma3 = 40 MPa."),
        EX("Worked example 2: stress on a plane", intro="sigma1 = 100 MPa and sigma3 = 40 MPa. Find the normal and shear stress on a plane whose normal is at 30 degrees to sigma1, and the greatest shear stress.", steps=[
            S("Centre and radius", math=r"c = \frac{100 + 40}{2} = 70, \quad r = \frac{100 - 40}{2} = 30"),
            S("Normal stress", math=r"\sigma_n = 70 + 30\cos 60^{\circ} = 70 + 15 = 85\text{ MPa}"),
            S("Shear stress", math=r"\tau = 30 \sin 60^{\circ} = 26.0\text{ MPa}"),
            S("Greatest shear stress", math=r"\tau_{max} = r = 30\text{ MPa at } 2\theta = 90^{\circ}", text="That is on planes at 45 degrees to sigma1."),
        ], check=r"30 \times 0.866 = 25.98"),
        H("Strain"),
        D("**Extension** e is the change in length divided by the original length (negative for shortening). The **stretch** S = l/l0 = 1 + e. **Shear strain** gamma = tan psi, where psi is the **angular shear**: the change in angle of a line that was originally perpendicular to a reference direction.", title="Measuring strain", formula=r"e = \frac{l - l_0}{l_0} \qquad \gamma = \tan\psi"),
        KEY("The strain ellipse and ellipsoid", "A circle drawn on an undeformed rock becomes an ellipse after homogeneous strain.", cards=[
            ["Strain ellipsoid", "In three dimensions a sphere becomes an ellipsoid with axes X (longest) at least Y at least Z (shortest)."],
            ["Shapes", "Prolate (cigar, X much longer: constrictional strain), oblate (pancake, Z much shorter: flattening), or plane strain (Y unchanged)."],
            ["Pure shear", "Coaxial: the axes of strain stay parallel to the same material lines; for example vertical flattening of a layer."],
            ["Simple shear", "Non-coaxial: like sliding a pack of cards; the strain axes rotate as strain increases. Typical of shear zones."]]),
        EX("Worked example 3: deformed ooids", intro="Originally spherical ooids in a deformed limestone are now ellipses 3 mm long and 1 mm wide in a section. Assuming no change in area, find the strain ratio, the original diameter and the extensions along X and Z.", steps=[
            S("Strain ratio", math=r"R_s = \frac{X}{Z} = \frac{3}{1} = 3"),
            S("Original diameter (constant area)", math=r"d_0 = \sqrt{3 \times 1} = 1.73\text{ mm}"),
            S("Extension along X", math=r"e_X = \frac{3 - 1.73}{1.73} = +0.73 \;(73\%\text{ lengthening})"),
            S("Extension along Z", math=r"e_Z = \frac{1 - 1.73}{1.73} = -0.42 \;(42\%\text{ shortening})"),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{3} = 1.732"),
        T("Evaluation: strain markers such as ooids, pebbles, reduction spots and fossils (for example distorted brachiopods or trilobites) give real strain values, but they assume the markers had a known original shape and deformed like the rock around them. Stiff pebbles in soft mud deform less than their matrix, so they underestimate the strain."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing stress with strain", "Stress is the force per area (a cause, in MPa); strain is the change of shape or size (an effect, with no units). Folds and cleavage record strain; we infer the stress."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to double the angle", "On the Mohr circle a plane at angle theta plots at 2 theta. Planes of greatest shear stress are at 45 degrees to sigma1, which is 90 degrees on the circle."),
        QUIZ("sigma1 = 120 MPa and sigma3 = 60 MPa. What is the greatest shear stress in the rock?", ["30 MPa", "60 MPa", "90 MPa", "180 MPa"], 0, "The greatest shear stress equals the radius, (120 - 60)/2 = 30 MPa.", hint="It is the radius of the Mohr circle.", feedback=["", "60 MPa is the differential stress, twice the radius.", "90 MPa is the centre of the circle (mean of sigma1 and sigma3).", "180 MPa is the sum of the two stresses."]),
        QUIZ("A line 50 cm long is shortened to 40 cm. Its extension is", ["+0.20", "-0.20", "0.80", "-0.25"], 1, "e = (40 - 50)/50 = -0.20: 20 per cent shortening.", hint="Divide the change by the original length.", feedback=["Shortening gives a negative extension.", "", "0.80 is the stretch S, not the extension.", "That divides by the new length, 40, instead of the original 50."]),
        TIP("Stress, strain and the Mohr circle are tested in Paper 1 calculations and definitions and in Paper 2 essays on rock deformation, where you should link differential stress to folds, faults and cleavage. Draw and label a Mohr circle neatly if a question asks for one.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Distinguish between lithostatic and differential stress and state which causes folding.", "Lithostatic stress is the equal, all-round stress caused by the weight of the overlying rock (sigma = rho g h); it changes volume only. Differential stress is the difference sigma1 - sigma3 between the greatest and least principal stresses; it changes the shape of rock and causes folding and faulting."),
        PQ("sigma1 = 80 MPa and sigma3 = 20 MPa. Calculate the normal and shear stress on a plane whose normal makes 60 degrees with sigma1.", "Centre = 50 MPa, radius = 30 MPa, 2 theta = 120 degrees. Normal stress = 50 + 30 cos 120 = 50 - 15 = 35 MPa. Shear stress = 30 sin 120 = 26.0 MPa."),
        PQ("Reduction spots in a slate, originally spherical, are now ellipses 4 cm long and 1 cm wide. Assuming no area change, find the strain ratio and the extensions along X and Z.", "Rs = 4/1 = 4. Original diameter = square root of (4 x 1) = 2 cm. eX = (4 - 2)/2 = +1.0 (100 per cent lengthening). eZ = (1 - 2)/2 = -0.5 (50 per cent shortening)."),
        PQ("Distinguish pure shear from simple shear.", "Both can produce the same strain ellipse. In pure shear (coaxial) the principal strain axes stay parallel to the same material lines throughout, as in flattening of a layer. In simple shear (non-coaxial) one set of planes slides past each other like cards in a pack and the strain axes rotate as strain increases, as in a shear zone."),
        SUM("Stress = force/area; on a plane it has normal and shear parts.",
            "Principal stresses sigma1 at least sigma2 at least sigma3; lithostatic stress rho g h changes volume, differential stress changes shape.",
            "Mohr circle: centre (sigma1 + sigma3)/2, radius (sigma1 - sigma3)/2; a plane plots at 2 theta.",
            "Greatest shear stress equals the radius, on planes at 45 degrees to sigma1.",
            "Strain: extension e = change/original; stretch S = 1 + e; shear strain gamma = tan psi.",
            "Strain ellipsoid X at least Y at least Z; pure shear is coaxial, simple shear non-coaxial."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Brittle and ductile deformation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Brittle and ductile deformation",
    title="Brittle and ductile deformation: stress-strain curves and failure",
    slug="gyu1-brittle-ductile-deformation",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Rocks deform elastically, then either break (brittle) or flow (ductile). Temperature, confining pressure, strain rate, fluids and rock type decide which. The Mohr-Coulomb criterion predicts when and at what angle a rock fails, and pore-fluid pressure makes failure easier.",
    objectives=[
        "Interpret stress-strain curves for brittle and ductile behaviour and calculate Young's modulus",
        "Explain how temperature, confining pressure, strain rate, fluids and lithology control behaviour, and the brittle-ductile transition",
        "Apply the Mohr-Coulomb failure criterion, including effective stress, and predict the angle of shear fractures",
        "Relate competence contrasts to structures such as boudinage and folds",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Elastic deformation", "Young's modulus", "Yield point", "Brittle", "Ductile", "Confining pressure",
                  "Strain rate", "Brittle-ductile transition", "Mohr-Coulomb criterion", "Pore-fluid pressure", "Competence", "Boudinage"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("The same granite that shatters under a hammer in a quarry near Yaoundé can be folded like soft clay deep in a mountain belt. Whether a rock breaks or flows does not depend only on the rock: it depends on the conditions. This lesson explains why, and gives you the failure criterion engineers use to judge the stability of slopes, tunnels and dam foundations."),
        D("**Elastic** strain disappears when the stress is removed; stress is proportional to strain (Hooke's law) and the constant is **Young's modulus** E. **Ductile** (plastic) strain is permanent deformation without visible fracturing. **Brittle** deformation is failure by fracturing, with loss of cohesion.", title="Three kinds of behaviour", formula=r"E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon}"),
        IMG("algyu1-stressstrain.svg", alt="Stress against strain. A steep orange line for a brittle rock rises in a straight line and ends at a cross marked fracture. A blue curve for a ductile rock rises in a straight elastic line to a yield point, then curves on to large permanent strain.", caption="Figure 1. Stress-strain curves for a brittle and a ductile rock (schematic)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: Young's modulus", intro="A sandstone cylinder shortens elastically by a strain of 0.001 when the stress is 50 MPa. Find Young's modulus.", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"E = \frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"E = \frac{50 \times 10^{6}}{0.001} = 5.0 \times 10^{10}\text{ Pa}"),
            S("Answer", math=r"E = 50\text{ GPa}"),
        ], check=r"50\times10^6/10^{-3} = 5\times10^{10}"),
        KEY("What makes a rock brittle or ductile?", "Each factor pushes the behaviour one way.", cards=[
            ["Temperature", "Higher temperature makes atoms and dislocations move more easily: more ductile."],
            ["Confining pressure", "High confining pressure (depth) stops cracks opening: more ductile and stronger."],
            ["Strain rate", "Slow loading allows creep and flow: more ductile. Fast loading (an earthquake, a hammer blow) favours fracture."],
            ["Pore-fluid pressure", "High fluid pressure in the pores pushes grains apart and reduces the effective confining pressure: more brittle. Water also weakens minerals and helps pressure solution."],
            ["Lithology", "Quartz-rich rocks and limestones become ductile at lower temperatures than feldspar- or olivine-rich rocks; shale and salt are weak; massive sandstone and dolerite are strong (**competent**)."]]),
        T("In continental crust the upper part deforms by brittle faulting and the lower part by ductile flow. The change, the **brittle-ductile transition**, is often placed at about 10 to 15 km, around 300 degrees C for quartz-rich rocks; most continental earthquakes start above it. The depth depends on the geothermal gradient and rock type, so check your textbook for the values it uses."),
        EX("Worked example 2: depth of the transition", intro="Take the surface temperature as 25 degrees C, a geothermal gradient of 25 degrees C per km, and brittle-ductile behaviour changing at 300 degrees C. At what depth is the transition?", steps=[
            S("Temperature rise needed", math=r"300 - 25 = 275\,{}^\circ\text{C}"),
            S("Depth", math=r"\frac{275}{25} = 11\text{ km}"),
            S("Comment", text="A steeper gradient (for example near a volcanic line) would raise the transition to shallower depth."),
        ], check=r"25 + 25 \times 11 = 300"),
        H("When does a rock break? The Mohr-Coulomb criterion"),
        D("A rock fails in shear when the shear stress on some plane reaches its shear strength, which rises with the normal stress pressing the plane together. C is the **cohesion** and mu the **coefficient of internal friction** (mu = tan phi, phi the angle of internal friction).", title="Mohr-Coulomb failure criterion", formula=r"\tau = C + \mu\,\sigma_n"),
        T("On a Mohr diagram this is a straight line, the failure envelope. A rock fails when its Mohr circle grows until it touches the envelope. The touching point shows that shear fractures form at an angle of 45 - phi/2 degrees to sigma1, about 30 degrees for typical rocks, so two conjugate sets of fractures make an acute angle of about 60 degrees bisected by sigma1. The idea of failure on a plane under friction is usually credited to Coulomb, and the circle to Otto Mohr."),
        D("Fluid in the pores pushes outwards and cancels part of the normal stress. The **effective normal stress** is the normal stress minus the pore-fluid pressure Pf. Raising Pf moves the Mohr circle towards the failure envelope without changing its size.", title="Effective stress", formula=r"\sigma_n' = \sigma_n - P_f"),
        EX("Worked example 3: will the fault slip?", intro="A fault plane has C = 10 MPa and mu = 0.6. The normal stress on it is 50 MPa and the shear stress 30 MPa. (a) Is it stable when dry? (b) Is it stable if the pore-fluid pressure rises to 20 MPa?", steps=[
            S("Dry strength", math=r"\tau_{crit} = 10 + 0.6 \times 50 = 40\text{ MPa}", text="30 MPa is less than 40 MPa: stable."),
            S("Effective normal stress", math=r"\sigma_n' = 50 - 20 = 30\text{ MPa}"),
            S("Wet strength", math=r"\tau_{crit} = 10 + 0.6 \times 30 = 28\text{ MPa}"),
            S("Conclusion", text="30 MPa now exceeds 28 MPa: the fault slips. This is how filling a reservoir or injecting fluids underground can trigger earthquakes."),
        ], check=r"10 + 18 = 28"),
        EX("Worked example 4: angle of shear fractures", intro="A rock has mu = 0.6. At what angle to sigma1 will shear fractures form?", steps=[
            S("Angle of internal friction", math=r"\phi = \tan^{-1} 0.6 = 31.0^{\circ}"),
            S("Fracture angle", math=r"\theta = 45 - \frac{\phi}{2} = 45 - 15.5 = 29.5^{\circ}"),
            S("Meaning", text="Conjugate fractures at about 30 degrees either side of sigma1, so the acute angle between them (about 59 degrees) contains sigma1."),
        ], check=r"45 - 30.96/2 = 29.5"),
        KEY("Competence in layered rocks", "Layers of different strength deform differently.", cards=[
            ["Boudinage", "A competent layer (sandstone, quartz vein) in weak rock is stretched until it breaks or necks into sausage-shaped boudins."],
            ["Buckle folds", "A competent layer shortened along its length buckles into folds; weak layers between flow to fill the space."],
            ["Tension gashes", "En-echelon extension fractures filled with quartz or calcite form in brittle layers, often in shear zones."]]),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a rock is simply 'brittle' or 'ductile'", "Behaviour depends on conditions. The same limestone is brittle in a quarry face and ductile at depth. Always state the conditions: temperature, confining pressure, strain rate and fluid pressure."),
        MISTAKE("Saying fluids make rocks stronger", "High pore-fluid pressure lowers the effective normal stress and makes brittle failure easier. Water also weakens many minerals."),
        QUIZ("Which change would make a rock more likely to behave in a ductile way?", ["Faster strain rate", "Lower temperature", "Higher confining pressure", "Higher pore-fluid pressure"], 2, "Confining pressure suppresses cracking, favouring ductile flow.", hint="Think of deep rock.", feedback=["Fast loading favours fracture.", "Cold rock is more brittle.", "", "High pore pressure lowers effective stress and favours brittle failure."]),
        QUIZ("A rock has C = 5 MPa and mu = 0.7. What shear stress is needed for failure on a plane with effective normal stress 50 MPa?", ["35 MPa", "40 MPa", "55 MPa", "61 MPa"], 1, "tau = 5 + 0.7 x 50 = 40 MPa.", hint="Use tau = C + mu sigma_n.", feedback=["35 MPa leaves out the cohesion.", "", "55 MPa adds C to the normal stress without multiplying by mu.", "61 MPa uses a normal stress of 80 MPa."]),
        TIP("Paper 1 can test the factors controlling brittle and ductile behaviour and simple Coulomb calculations; Paper 2 essays on folds, faults and earthquakes need them as explanation (the Board's report lists slip on faults, elastic rebound and human activities such as filling reservoirs and fluid injection among the causes of earthquakes).", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Explain why rocks near the Earth's surface usually fault while rocks at 20 km depth usually fold.", "Near the surface temperature and confining pressure are low and the rocks are cold and strong, so they fail by fracture (brittle). At 20 km temperature is several hundred degrees and confining pressure is high; minerals deform by creep and cracks cannot open, so rocks flow and fold (ductile). The slow strain rate of tectonic deformation also favours ductile behaviour at depth."),
        PQ("A fault has C = 10 MPa and mu = 0.6, normal stress 70 MPa and shear stress 40 MPa. What pore-fluid pressure would make it slip?", "Slip when 40 = 10 + 0.6 sigma_n', so sigma_n' = 30/0.6 = 50 MPa. Pore pressure = 70 - 50 = 20 MPa. Any pore pressure above 20 MPa causes slip."),
        PQ("A rock has an angle of internal friction of 40 degrees. Predict the angle between conjugate shear fractures and state which principal stress bisects the acute angle.", "Each fracture is at 45 - 40/2 = 25 degrees to sigma1, so the acute angle between the conjugate set is 50 degrees and it is bisected by sigma1 (the obtuse angle by sigma3)."),
        SUM("Elastic strain is recoverable (E = stress/strain); ductile strain is permanent flow; brittle failure is fracturing.",
            "Ductile behaviour is favoured by high temperature, high confining pressure, slow strain rate and weak minerals.",
            "The brittle-ductile transition in continental crust lies at roughly 10 to 15 km, depending on the geothermal gradient.",
            "Mohr-Coulomb: tau = C + mu sigma_n; shear fractures form at 45 - phi/2 (about 30 degrees) to sigma1.",
            "Pore-fluid pressure lowers the effective normal stress and can trigger faulting.",
            "Competence contrasts give boudinage, buckle folds and tension gashes."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Folds
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Folds",
    title="Folds: description, Ramsay classification and map evidence",
    slug="gyu1-folds-classification-map-evidence",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Describe folds by interlimb angle, axial plane and plunge; classify them by dip isogons (Ramsay classes 1B, 2 and 3); explain buckling and flexural slip; read anticlines and synclines on a map; and name the major structures associated with folds.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe a fold using interlimb angle, axial plane attitude, plunge and symmetry",
        "Classify folds by dip isogons into Ramsay classes 1, 2 and 3 and use the thickness ratio",
        "Identify anticlines and synclines on a map using dip symbols and the age of the core beds, and give six-figure grid references",
        "Calculate shortening and explain fold mechanics; name the major structures associated with folds",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Interlimb angle", "Axial plane", "Plunge", "Dip isogon", "Ramsay classification", "Buckling", "Flexural slip",
                  "Similar fold", "Parallel fold", "Salt dome", "Nappe", "Pericline"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("At O Level you named anticlines and synclines. The Advanced Level examiners go further: they expect you to describe a fold in words, to find folds on a map from evidence, and to explain how folds form. In the Board's 2023 practical paper, most candidates could not locate the folds on the map with a six-figure grid reference, gave a four-figure reference instead, and some started with the northing instead of the easting. This lesson builds the skill step by step."),
        KEY("Describing a fold", "Give these in order.", cards=[
            ["Interlimb angle", "The angle between the two limbs. Gentle 180 to 120 degrees, open 120 to 70, close 70 to 30, tight 30 to 0, isoclinal 0 (check the limits in your textbook)."],
            ["Axial plane", "Vertical (upright), inclined, or nearly horizontal (recumbent). An inclined axial plane gives an asymmetrical fold; overturned when one limb has been rotated past vertical."],
            ["Plunge", "The angle between the fold axis and the horizontal. A plunging fold shows a curved, nose-shaped outcrop pattern."],
            ["Shape in profile", "Rounded or angular (chevron or box), and whether the thickness of layers changes round the fold."]]),
        D("Where beds dip towards a point from all sides the structure is a **basin**; where they dip away from a point it is a **dome**. Doubly plunging folds with a dome or basin shape are **periclines**.", title="Dome, basin and pericline"),
        H("Ramsay classification"),
        T("In a cross-section, a **dip isogon** joins points of equal dip on the upper and lower surfaces of a folded layer. Ramsay (1967) classified folds by how these lines behave and by the thickness ratio t' = (thickness at dip alpha, measured at right angles to the layer) / (thickness at the hinge)."),
        IMG("algyu1-ramsay.svg", alt="Three fold profiles with two surfaces and orange dashed dip isogons. Class 1B: isogons converge towards the core, thickness constant. Class 2: isogons parallel to the axial surface. Class 3: isogons diverge towards the core.", caption="Figure 1. Ramsay classes 1B, 2 and 3 (schematic)."),
        KEY("The three classes", "Learn the isogon pattern and what it means.", cards=[
            ["Class 1 (isogons converge towards the core)", "Subclass 1B (parallel folds): thickness measured at right angles to the layer is constant, t' = 1. Typical of competent layers such as sandstone that buckle."],
            ["Class 2 (similar folds)", "Isogons are parallel to the axial surface, so the thickness measured along the axial direction is constant; the layer is thinner on the limbs: t' = cos alpha. Typical of ductile rock that flows."],
            ["Class 3", "Isogons diverge towards the core; the layer is thickest on the limbs. Rare in nature."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: thickness on a similar fold", intro="A layer is 10 m thick at the hinge of a Class 2 fold. What is its thickness measured at right angles to the layer where the limb dips at 60 degrees?", steps=[
            S("Class 2 rule", math=r"t' = \cos\alpha"),
            S("Thickness ratio", math=r"t' = \cos 60^{\circ} = 0.50"),
            S("Thickness", math=r"10 \times 0.50 = 5\text{ m}"),
            S("Compare", text="A parallel (Class 1B) fold would still be 10 m thick on the limb."),
        ], check=r"10 \times 0.5 = 5"),
        H("How folds form"),
        KEY("Fold mechanisms", "Folding is usually a combination.", cards=[
            ["Buckling", "A competent layer shortened along its length buckles at a wavelength set by its thickness and its stiffness contrast with the layers around it. Gives parallel folds with weaker rock flowing into the hinges."],
            ["Flexural slip and flow", "Beds slide over each other like cards in a pack, giving slickensides on bedding surfaces and bedding-parallel veins. Layers keep their thickness."],
            ["Passive folding and shear", "Layers are folded by flow without controlling it: similar folds in a ductile shear zone."],
            ["Drape and forced folds", "Beds drape over a rising fault block or intrusion; a rising salt body arches the beds above it."]]),
        EX("Worked example 2: shortening and interlimb angle", intro="A symmetrical upright fold has both limbs dipping at 30 degrees. (a) Find the interlimb angle. (b) A bed 9.24 km long when unfolded is folded into a chevron fold spanning 8 km. Find the shortening.", steps=[
            S("Interlimb angle", math=r"180 - (30 + 30) = 120^{\circ}", text="At the boundary between gentle and open."),
            S("Shortening", math=r"\frac{9.24 - 8}{9.24} \times 100 = 13.4\%"),
        ], check=r"1.24/9.24 = 0.134"),
        H("Folds on a map"),
        IMG("algyu1-foldmap.svg", alt="Schematic map, eastings 40 to 48, northings 60 to 66. Vertical strips C, B, A, B, C, B from west to east. Dip symbols show beds dipping away from strip A and towards the middle of the wide C strip. Section line X to Y runs west to east at northing 63. Dashed lines mark the fold axes.", caption="Figure 2. An invented map extract on flat ground. Beds A (oldest), B and C (youngest). Grid numbers are in kilometres."),
        KEY("Evidence for a fold on a map", "Use these steps in order in an answer.", cards=[
            ["1. Outcrop pattern", "The same bed repeats symmetrically on both sides of an axis (here B-A-B and C-B-A-B-C)."],
            ["2. Age of the core", "Oldest bed in the core of an anticline (A at easting 43); youngest bed in the core of a syncline (C at easting 46)."],
            ["3. Dip symbols", "In an anticline they point away from the axis; in a syncline towards it. The Board noted candidates who wrote 'beds moving away' instead of dip symbols dipping away."],
            ["4. Axial trace", "Give the position and trend with grid references and cardinal points: write north to south, not N-S."]]),
        EX("Worked example 3: describing the map", intro="Use Figure 2 to describe the fold at easting 43 and give the true thickness of bed B.", steps=[
            S("Name", text="The A strip is the oldest bed and the beds B on both sides dip away at 30 degrees: an **anticline**, with core bed A. It trends north to south."),
            S("Grid reference", text="The axis crosses northing 64.5 at easting 43.0: grid reference **430645** (easting first)."),
            S("Outcrop width of B", text="West limb: from easting 41.68 to 42.48 = 0.80 km on flat ground."),
            S("True thickness", math=r"t = w\sin\delta = 0.80 \times \sin 30^{\circ} = 0.40\text{ km} = 400\text{ m}"),
        ], check=r"0.8 \times 0.5 = 0.4"),
        H("Major structures associated with folds"),
        T("The Board's examiners reported that most candidates described anticlines and synclines when asked for the major structures associated with folds. The structures expected were: **salt domes** (diapirs), **gravity collapse structures**, **nappe folds and thrust sheets**, **valley bulges and cambering**, and **periclines** (domes and basins). Minor structures such as boudinage and tension gashes do not answer such a question."),
        MISTAKE("Using four-figure references", "Locate a fold with a six-figure grid reference: three digits for the easting, then three digits for the northing. Easting first, always."),
        MISTAKE("Saying antiform and synform for anticline and syncline", "Antiform and synform describe shape only, with no knowledge of age. Name anticlines and synclines when the core beds are known (oldest in the anticline core, youngest in the syncline core)."),
        QUIZ("A map shows the oldest bed in the centre of a strip with beds dipping away on each side. The fold is", ["A syncline", "An anticline", "A dome only", "A monocline"], 1, "Dip away from the axis with oldest rock in the core is an anticline.", hint="Where is the oldest bed?", feedback=["A syncline has youngest rock in the core and limbs dipping inward.", "", "A dome dips away in all directions, not on two sides only.", "A monocline is a single step in otherwise flat beds."]),
        QUIZ("In a Class 2 (similar) fold the thickness ratio t' on a limb with dip 45 degrees is", ["1.00", "0.71", "0.50", "1.41"], 1, "t' = cos 45 degrees = 0.71.", hint="Class 2: t' = cos alpha.", feedback=["t' = 1.00 on every limb is Class 1B (parallel folds).", "", "0.50 is cos 60 degrees.", "A ratio above 1 would mean the layer is thicker on the limb, as in Class 3."]),
        TIP("The Board's 2023 practical paper used a map with folds: candidates had to locate them with six-figure references, name them as anticline or syncline, give the core beds and the evidence, and draw a section across a line. Folds also appear in Paper 1 and in Paper 2 essays on rock deformation.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("List four items you would include to describe a fold fully.", "Interlimb angle (gentle, open, close, tight, isoclinal); attitude of the axial plane (upright, inclined, overturned, recumbent); plunge of the axis; shape and thickness change (rounded or angular, parallel or similar); trend of the axial trace on a map."),
        PQ("In Figure 2, state the name, core bed, trend and evidence for the fold at easting 46.", "It is a syncline. The core bed is C, the youngest. It trends north to south. Evidence: bed C is in the middle with older bed B on both sides, and the dip symbols point towards the axis (the west limb dips east and the east limb dips west)."),
        PQ("A competent sandstone 20 m thick is buckled into a Class 1B fold and a shale layer 20 m thick at its hinge is flowed into a Class 2 fold. Compare the thickness of each on limbs dipping 60 degrees.", "Sandstone (1B): thickness is constant at right angles to the layer, so 20 m. Shale (Class 2): t' = cos 60 degrees = 0.5, so 10 m."),
        SUM("Describe a fold by interlimb angle, axial plane, plunge and shape.",
            "Ramsay: Class 1B parallel (t' = 1), Class 2 similar (t' = cos alpha), Class 3 rare.",
            "Folds form by buckling, flexural slip and flow; shortening = (original - final)/original.",
            "On a map: repeated bed, core age, dip symbols away (anticline) or towards (syncline), six-figure grid reference with easting first.",
            "Major associated structures: salt domes, gravity collapse structures, nappes, valley bulges and periclines."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Faults and shear zones
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Faults and shear zones",
    title="Faults and shear zones: kinematics, stress and geometry",
    slug="gyu1-faults-shear-zones-kinematics",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Classify faults by slip (normal, reverse, thrust, strike-slip, oblique), link them to principal stresses (Anderson), calculate throw, heave and displacement, recognise faults on maps and in the field, and contrast brittle faults with ductile shear zones.",
    objectives=[
        "Classify faults by the sense of slip and relate each type to the principal stress directions",
        "Calculate throw, heave and net displacement and the dip of faults predicted by Coulomb failure",
        "Recognise faults on a map and in the field, and state the trend of a fault correctly",
        "Describe ductile shear zones, calculate shear strain and distinguish them from brittle faults",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Hanging wall", "Footwall", "Throw", "Heave", "Net slip", "Normal fault", "Thrust", "Strike-slip fault",
                  "Horst and graben", "Listric fault", "Slickenside", "Fault breccia", "Mylonite", "Shear zone", "Elastic rebound"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon lies on an old continent, but it is not free of faults: the Mamfe basin and the Benue trough record ancient rifting, and the Cameroon Volcanic Line follows a long zone of crustal weakness. To read these structures, and to answer the map questions of the practical paper, you need a precise language for faults. The Board's 2023 report noted that many candidates described folds when asked for faults and could not state the trend of the faults on a map."),
        D("A **fault** is a fracture along which the rock on one side has moved relative to the other. The **hanging wall** is the block above an inclined fault plane and the **footwall** the block below it. **Net slip** (displacement) is the true distance between two points that were once together. **Throw** is the vertical component and **heave** the horizontal component of that displacement, for a dip-slip fault measured in a section at right angles to the strike.", title="Fault, hanging wall and footwall"),
        IMG("algyu1-fault.svg", alt="Cross-section of a normal fault dipping at 60 degrees to the right. A green marker bed is cut by the orange fault plane; the hanging wall on the right has dropped. Throw is the vertical offset and heave the horizontal offset.", caption="Figure 1. Throw and heave on a normal fault. The hanging wall is the block above the fault plane."),
        F(r"\text{throw} = d\sin\delta \qquad \text{heave} = d\cos\delta \qquad \text{heave} = \frac{\text{throw}}{\tan\delta}", title="Pure dip-slip fault: d net slip, delta dip of the fault"),
        KEY("Types of fault", "Name by the movement of the hanging wall, or by the horizontal movement for strike-slip faults.", cards=[
            ["Normal", "Hanging wall moves down. Extension. Dip usually 50 to 70 degrees. Lengthens the crust. Forms horsts and grabens and rift valleys."],
            ["Reverse and thrust", "Hanging wall moves up. Compression. A reverse fault dips more than about 45 degrees; a **thrust** dips less than that (often 10 to 30 degrees). Shortens and thickens the crust; repeats beds."],
            ["Strike-slip", "Blocks move horizontally along the strike. Near-vertical dip. **Dextral** (right-lateral): the opposite block moves to the right as you face the fault. **Sinistral**: to the left. Transform plate boundaries are strike-slip."],
            ["Oblique-slip", "Both dip-slip and strike-slip components."]]),
        H("Faults and stress: Anderson's classification"),
        T("Near the surface one principal stress is vertical, because the ground is free. E. M. Anderson (1905, 1951) showed that the orientation of the other two stresses decides the fault type. Coulomb failure (previous lesson) predicts shear fractures at about 30 degrees to sigma1:"),
        KEY("Anderson's three cases", "Rely on the vertical stress.", cards=[
            ["Normal fault", "sigma1 vertical (the weight of the rock), sigma3 horizontal. Fault dip = 90 - 30 = about 60 degrees."],
            ["Thrust fault", "sigma3 vertical, sigma1 horizontal. Fault dip about 30 degrees."],
            ["Strike-slip fault", "sigma2 vertical, sigma1 and sigma3 horizontal. Vertical fault planes at about 30 degrees to sigma1."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: throw, heave and net slip", intro="A normal fault dips at 60 degrees. A bed is offset by a throw of 120 m. Find the heave and the net slip, assuming pure dip-slip.", steps=[
            S("Heave", math=r"\text{heave} = \frac{120}{\tan 60^{\circ}} = 69.3\text{ m}"),
            S("Net slip", math=r"d = \frac{\text{throw}}{\sin 60^{\circ}} = \frac{120}{0.866} = 138.6\text{ m}"),
            S("Check", math=r"\sqrt{120^2 + 69.3^2} = 138.6"),
            S("Meaning", text="The heave is the amount of horizontal extension of the crust across the fault."),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{14400 + 4800} = 138.6"),
        EX("Worked example 2: dip from the failure criterion", intro="A rock has a coefficient of internal friction of 0.6. Predict the dip of a normal fault and a thrust.", steps=[
            S("Friction angle", math=r"\phi = \tan^{-1} 0.6 = 31.0^{\circ}"),
            S("Angle to sigma1", math=r"45 - \phi/2 = 29.5^{\circ}"),
            S("Normal fault (sigma1 vertical)", math=r"\text{dip} = 90 - 29.5 = 60.5^{\circ}"),
            S("Thrust (sigma1 horizontal)", math=r"\text{dip} = 29.5^{\circ}"),
        ], check=r"90 - 29.5 = 60.5"),
        H("Fault patterns and recognition"),
        KEY("Common patterns", "Learn the ones in rift basins and mountain belts.", cards=[
            ["Horst and graben", "A horst is a block raised between two normal faults dipping away from each other; a graben is a block dropped between faults dipping towards each other. Rift valleys are large grabens."],
            ["Listric faults", "Normal faults that flatten with depth; the hanging wall rotates and beds tilt towards the fault. Common on passive margins and in delta basins."],
            ["Thrust belts", "Series of thrusts that stack slices of rock (imbricate thrusts); a very large thrust sheet moved far is a **nappe**."]]),
        KEY("Evidence for faults", "Use at least three in an answer.", cards=[
            ["On a map", "Beds or contacts abruptly cut off and displaced; repeated or missing beds along a traverse; a straight line cutting across the outcrop pattern; a sudden change in dip across a line."],
            ["In the field", "Slickensides (polished, grooved surfaces); fault breccia and fault gouge; drag folds; mineral veins; a scarp, springs along a line."],
            ["Earthquakes", "Faults can slip suddenly: the rocks on either side bend elastically under stress, then rebound when the fault breaks (elastic rebound), releasing seismic waves."]]),
        T("When you describe a fault on a map, give its location (using grid references and cardinal points such as in the north-east), its **trend** written from one direction to another, for example north to south, not N-S (the Board noted candidates writing S-N for N-S), and the evidence."),
        H("Ductile shear zones"),
        D("A **shear zone** is a tabular zone of rock in which deformation is concentrated by ductile flow rather than by a single fracture. At depth, in the ductile part of the crust, rocks are deformed by crystal-plastic creep and a fault becomes a broad zone. Rocks within it are **mylonites**: fine-grained, strongly foliated and often lineated, with **S-C fabrics** that show the sense of shear.", title="Shear zone and mylonite"),
        F(r"\gamma = \frac{\text{displacement}}{\text{width of shear zone}} = \tan\psi", title="Simple shear strain (psi angular shear)"),
        EX("Worked example 3: strain in a shear zone", intro="Across a shear zone 10 m wide, markers are displaced 50 m. Find the shear strain and the angular shear.", steps=[
            S("Shear strain", math=r"\gamma = \frac{50}{10} = 5"),
            S("Angular shear", math=r"\psi = \tan^{-1} 5 = 78.7^{\circ}"),
            S("Meaning", text="The strain is high: lines originally perpendicular to the zone have rotated almost into it."),
        ], check=r"\tan 78.7^{\circ} = 5.0"),
        KEY("Brittle fault or ductile shear zone?", "Depth and conditions decide.", cards=[
            ["Brittle fault", "Sharp plane, breccia or gouge, cataclasite; shallow crust; slip in earthquakes."],
            ["Ductile shear zone", "Gradual change in foliation across the zone, mylonite; deep, hot crust; slow creep."],
            ["Transition", "Many large shear zones have both: ductile fabrics formed at depth are later cut by brittle faults as the rocks are uplifted."]]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up throw and heave", "Throw is the vertical offset, heave the horizontal offset. Heave = throw / tan(dip) for a dip-slip fault. They must not be confused with the net slip (displacement) along the plane."),
        MISTAKE("Calling every fault with a downthrown side a normal fault", "A reverse fault also has a downthrown footwall. The type depends on the movement of the hanging wall: down is normal, up is reverse."),
        QUIZ("A reverse fault dipping at 35 degrees is usually called a", ["Normal fault", "Thrust", "Strike-slip fault", "Graben"], 1, "A reverse fault with a gentle dip (under about 45 degrees) is a thrust.", hint="Think of dip angle and compression.", feedback=["A normal fault has the hanging wall moving down.", "", "A strike-slip fault is near vertical.", "A graben is a down-dropped block between two normal faults."]),
        QUIZ("In Anderson's scheme a normal fault forms when", ["sigma1 is horizontal", "sigma1 is vertical", "sigma2 is vertical", "sigma3 is vertical"], 1, "Extension: the weight of the rock is the greatest stress and sigma3 is horizontal.", hint="Which stress is vertical when the crust is stretched?", feedback=["Horizontal sigma1 gives thrusting.", "", "Vertical sigma2 gives strike-slip faults.", "Vertical sigma3 gives thrusts."]),
        TIP("Faults are examined in Paper 1, in map questions of Paper 3 (locating and describing faults, with grid references and trends) and in Paper 2 essays on rift valleys, earthquakes and plate tectonics. The Board's report lists the causes of earthquakes expected: fault slip with elastic rebound, volcanic activity, tectonic forces, underwater landslides, impacts, explosions and human activities such as mining, tunnel construction and filling reservoirs.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Distinguish between throw and heave and state the relationship between them for a fault dipping at an angle delta.", "Throw is the vertical component of the displacement of a bed across a fault; heave is the horizontal component. For a dip-slip fault heave = throw / tan(delta), so at a dip of 45 degrees they are equal and for steeper faults the heave is smaller than the throw."),
        PQ("A normal fault dips at 50 degrees and the heave of a bed is 50 m. Calculate the throw and the net slip.", "Throw = heave x tan 50 = 50 x 1.192 = 59.6 m. Net slip = heave / cos 50 = 50 / 0.643 = 77.8 m."),
        PQ("Describe the evidence that would show that a fault on a map is a strike-slip fault rather than a dip-slip fault.", "Beds or contacts are displaced horizontally along a straight line that cuts across topography without being deflected; the fault trace is straight even across hills and valleys (steep or vertical dip); the same beds are on both sides with an offset, not repeated or missing; slickensides plunge shallowly; and the trend and offset sense (dextral or sinistral) can be given."),
        PQ("Essay plan: explain why normal faults dominate in rift valleys and thrusts in mountain belts.", "Plan: (1) Anderson's classification. (2) Rift valleys: crust is stretched, sigma1 is vertical, sigma3 horizontal, normal faults dip about 60 degrees, forming horsts and grabens and thinning the crust. (3) Mountain belts: compression, sigma1 horizontal, sigma3 vertical, thrusts dip about 30 degrees, thickening the crust, stacking thrust sheets and nappes. (4) Link to plate boundaries: divergent (rifts), convergent (belts). (5) Evaluate: strike-slip faults at transforms and oblique faults at oblique plate motion; faults reactivate."),
        SUM("Hanging wall is above the fault plane; throw is vertical, heave horizontal, net slip along the plane.",
            "Normal faults: hanging wall down, extension; reverse and thrust: hanging wall up, compression; strike-slip: horizontal.",
            "Anderson: sigma1 vertical gives normal faults (dip about 60), sigma3 vertical gives thrusts (about 30), sigma2 vertical gives strike-slip.",
            "Heave = throw / tan(dip); net slip = throw / sin(dip).",
            "Describe faults on maps with grid references, trend (north to south), evidence and type.",
            "Shear zones are ductile: mylonite, S-C fabrics, shear strain = displacement / width."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 12. Cleavage and foliation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Cleavage and foliation",
    title="Cleavage and foliation: how rocks split and what it tells us",
    slug="gyu1-cleavage-foliation",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Slaty cleavage, schistosity and gneissic banding form perpendicular to the greatest shortening. Learn how cleavage forms, how it relates to folds, how bedding-cleavage angles show the way up and the fold position, and how the slate-phyllite-schist-gneiss series records grade.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish cleavage, foliation, schistosity and gneissic banding and describe the main types of cleavage",
        "Explain how cleavage forms by rotation, pressure solution and recrystallization, perpendicular to the greatest shortening",
        "Use the bedding-cleavage relationship to find the fold position and whether beds are upright",
        "Describe slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss and migmatite by composition, texture and formation, and calculate shortening from deformed spots",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Foliation", "Slaty cleavage", "Crenulation cleavage", "Schistosity", "Gneissic banding", "Axial planar cleavage",
                  "Cleavage refraction", "Intersection lineation", "Bedding-cleavage relationship", "Mylonite"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Roofing slate splits into thin flat sheets, but not along the bedding of the original mud: it splits along a surface made by compression. In regions of low-grade metamorphism, including parts of old mountain belts, you can recognise the folds from the cleavage even where the beds are hard to see. The Board's examiners noticed that metamorphism is taught poorly: many candidates could not explain how foliated rocks formed or state whether the grade was low or high, and some listed granite as a foliated metamorphic rock. This lesson fixes that."),
        D("A **foliation** is any planar fabric in a rock produced by deformation or metamorphism: **cleavage** (closely spaced, parallel planes along which a rock splits) and the coarser **schistosity** and **gneissic banding**. A planar feature present at the time of deposition is **bedding** (S0). Foliations are numbered S1, S2 in order of formation.", title="Foliation and cleavage"),
        KEY("Types of cleavage and foliation", "From low to high grade; sizes of grains grow with grade.", cards=[
            ["Slaty cleavage (continuous)", "Very fine-grained; platy minerals (mica, chlorite) too small to see are aligned; splits into thin sheets. Low-grade metamorphism of mudrock."],
            ["Spaced and crenulation cleavage", "Cleavage domains (rich in insoluble minerals) alternate with microlithons. Crenulation cleavage folds an earlier cleavage into tiny wrinkles."],
            ["Phyllitic texture", "Slightly coarser; sheen of fine mica (sericite) on the surface."],
            ["Schistosity", "Medium to coarse grained; visible micas and other minerals (garnet, kyanite) aligned. Medium grade."],
            ["Gneissic banding", "Coarse grained; alternating light (quartz, feldspar) and dark (biotite, hornblende) bands. High grade."]]),
        H("How cleavage forms"),
        KEY("Mechanisms", "Several operate together.", cards=[
            ["Mechanical rotation", "Platy grains such as mica and chlorite rotate into the plane of flattening, like coins turned flat in mud."],
            ["Pressure solution", "Quartz and calcite dissolve where grains are squeezed together; the insoluble residue forms dark seams and the dissolved material is moved away or deposited in low-stress areas."],
            ["Recrystallization and new growth", "New mica grows with its flat faces perpendicular to the greatest stress. Heat helps."],
            ["Flattening of grains", "Grains are shortened parallel to sigma1 and lengthened in the cleavage plane."]]),
        T("In every case the cleavage plane lies at right angles to the greatest compressive stress (sigma1), so it is parallel to the XY plane of the strain ellipsoid, the plane of flattening. Cleavage is therefore a record of strain, not of the original bedding."),
        IMG("algyu1-cleavage.svg", alt="Cross-section of three folded layers (blue) with a vertical dashed axial plane and thin orange cleavage lines close to vertical, fanning slightly. At the hinge the cleavage is at right angles to bedding.", caption="Figure 1. Axial planar cleavage (orange, S1) cutting folded bedding (blue, S0). The dashed line is the axial plane."),
        EX("Worked example 1: shortening from reduction spots", intro="Spherical reduction spots in a slate appear as ellipses with axial ratio 4:1 in a section perpendicular to the cleavage and to the stretching direction. Assuming no area change, find the shortening perpendicular to the cleavage and the extension along the cleavage.", steps=[
            S("Original diameter", math=r"d_0 = \sqrt{4 \times 1} = 2\text{ (units)}"),
            S("Short axis", math=r"e_Z = \frac{1 - 2}{2} = -0.50", text="50 per cent shortening perpendicular to the cleavage."),
            S("Long axis", math=r"e_X = \frac{4 - 2}{2} = +1.0", text="100 per cent extension along the cleavage."),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{4} = 2"),
        H("Cleavage and folds"),
        T("Cleavage is commonly **axial planar**: parallel (or nearly parallel) to the axial plane of the folds formed in the same deformation. It is usually steeper than bedding on the **upright** limb of a fold and less steep than bedding on the **overturned** limb, when both dip in the same direction. The line where cleavage meets bedding is the **intersection lineation**, parallel to the fold axis. Together these let a geologist work out where the hinge lies without seeing it."),
        KEY("Bedding-cleavage relationships", "Use bedding and cleavage to read the fold.", cards=[
            ["Cleavage steeper than bedding (same dip direction)", "Beds are upright (younging up) on this limb. Always confirm with way-up criteria such as graded bedding. The angle between bedding and cleavage is smallest on the limbs and increases towards the hinge, where cleavage is nearly perpendicular to bedding."],
            ["Cleavage less steep than bedding", "Beds are overturned on that limb."],
            ["Bedding and cleavage at right angles", "You are near the hinge."],
            ["Bedding and cleavage parallel", "On a limb of a very tight fold, or where cleavage has been transposed into bedding."]]),
        T("**Cleavage refraction**: when cleavage crosses layers of different competence, its angle changes, usually becoming closer to the bedding in the mudrock and steeper in the sandstone, giving a fan shaped pattern across the fold. Check your textbook for the exact patterns your syllabus expects."),
        EX("Worked example 2: upright or overturned?", intro="In a slate belt, bedding strikes north to south and dips 40 degrees east. Cleavage in the same outcrop strikes north to south and dips 70 degrees east. Are the beds upright or overturned, and what is the angle between bedding and cleavage?", steps=[
            S("Same strike and dip direction", text="Both dip east, so the rule applies."),
            S("Compare dips", text="Cleavage (70 degrees) is steeper than bedding (40 degrees): the beds are upright on this limb."),
            S("Angle between planes", math=r"70 - 40 = 30^{\circ}"),
            S("Intersection lineation", text="With the same strike, the lineation is horizontal and trends north to south; so the fold axis is horizontal, trending north to south."),
        ], check=r"70 - 40 = 30"),
        T("If the strikes differ, the intersection lineation plunges. For bedding 000 degrees dipping 40 degrees east and cleavage 020 degrees dipping 70 degrees south-east, a vector calculation (or a stereonet) gives a lineation plunging about 22 degrees towards 208 degrees: the fold axis plunges to the south-south-west."),
        H("The slate to gneiss series: model descriptions"),
        KEY("From shale to migmatite", "The Board's examiners asked for composition, texture and mode of formation. All are formed from shale (pelitic or argillaceous rock) by regional metamorphism.", cards=[
            ["Slate", "Chlorite, sericite (fine mica), quartz; very fine grained, slaty cleavage; low grade: low temperature and pressure."],
            ["Phyllite", "Sericite, chlorite, small quartz and feldspar; fine grained with silky sheen, phyllitic texture; low grade."],
            ["Schist", "Muscovite, biotite, chlorite, quartz, garnet, kyanite; medium to coarse grained, schistose texture; medium grade."],
            ["Gneiss", "Quartz, feldspar, biotite, hornblende, garnet; coarse grained, gneissic banding, often porphyroblastic; high grade."],
            ["Migmatite", "Quartz, feldspar, mica, hornblende, garnet; coarse grained; formed when partial melting (anatexis) occurs at very high temperature, mixing a dark metamorphic part with a light granitic part."]]),
        MISTAKE("Giving granite as a foliated metamorphic rock", "Granite is igneous (formed from cooling magma). Gneiss may look like granite but has gneissic banding and is metamorphic. Foliated rocks are slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss and migmatite."),
        MISTAKE("Treating mica and biotite as separate groups", "Mica is a group; biotite and muscovite are two micas. Say 'biotite mica' or list biotite and muscovite as members of the mica group."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing cleavage with bedding or with mineral cleavage", "Slaty cleavage is a rock fabric made by deformation. Mineral cleavage is the way a crystal splits along weak atomic planes. Bedding is a sedimentary layer."),
        QUIZ("A rock splits into thin flat sheets along surfaces that cut across the original bedding. These sheets reflect", ["Deposition of mud in thin layers", "Alignment of platy minerals perpendicular to the greatest stress", "Cooling of magma", "The crystal cleavage of calcite"], 1, "Slaty cleavage forms perpendicular to the greatest compression, by rotation and growth of mica and chlorite.", hint="Slate is a metamorphic rock formed by compression.", feedback=["That is bedding, which slaty cleavage cuts across.", "", "Cooling magma makes igneous textures.", "That is mineral cleavage, a crystal property."]),
        QUIZ("Which rock is formed at the highest metamorphic grade?", ["Slate", "Phyllite", "Schist", "Gneiss"], 3, "The series slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss records rising temperature and pressure.", hint="Grain size and banding grow with grade.", feedback=["Slate is low grade.", "Phyllite is low grade, slightly coarser.", "Schist is medium grade.", ""]),
        TIP("Foliated rocks are tested in Paper 1, in Paper 2 essays on metamorphism (the Board's 2023 report asked for the composition, texture and mode of formation of foliated rocks, with the parent rock and the grade) and in Paper 3 hand specimens such as gneiss. Always say shale or pelitic parent rock and low, medium or high grade.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Describe how slaty cleavage forms.", "Mud is buried and compressed in a mountain belt. Clay minerals are changed into fine mica and chlorite. Under compression the platy grains rotate and new mica grows with its flat faces perpendicular to the greatest stress; pressure solution removes quartz and calcite from grain contacts. The result is a continuous fabric of aligned minerals at right angles to the greatest compression and parallel to the axial plane of folds."),
        PQ("Spherical reduction spots in a slate are ellipses with axial ratio 9:1. Assuming no change in area, calculate the shortening perpendicular to the cleavage.", "Original diameter = square root of (9 x 1) = 3. Short axis = 1. Shortening = (3 - 1)/3 = 0.667, that is 66.7 per cent."),
        PQ("In an outcrop, bedding dips 60 degrees west and cleavage dips 35 degrees west with the same strike. State whether beds are upright and justify.", "Cleavage dips less steeply than bedding in the same direction, so the beds are overturned on this limb. The angle between them is 25 degrees. The result should be confirmed with way-up evidence such as graded bedding or cross-bedding."),
        PQ("Write a model description of a schist, giving parent rock, composition, texture and grade.", "Parent rock shale (pelitic rock). Composition: muscovite, biotite, chlorite, quartz, with garnet or kyanite. Texture: medium to coarse grained, schistose (visible aligned mica), foliated. Formed by medium-grade regional metamorphism at moderate temperature and pressure."),
        SUM("Foliation includes cleavage, schistosity and gneissic banding; cleavage is perpendicular to the greatest compression.",
            "Cleavage forms by rotation of platy minerals, pressure solution and recrystallization.",
            "Cleavage is usually axial planar; the intersection with bedding gives the fold axis.",
            "Cleavage steeper than bedding (same dip direction): upright; less steep: overturned.",
            "Shale gives slate, phyllite, schist, gneiss and migmatite as grade rises.",
            "Shortening from spots: Z/Z0 = 1/square root of axial ratio, assuming constant area."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 13. Stratum contours and three-point problems
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Stratum contours and three-point problems",
    title="Stratum contours and three-point problems",
    slug="gyu1-stratum-contours-three-point",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Stratum (structure) contours are lines of equal height on a bedding surface. Use them to find strike, dip and dip direction from three borehole heights, to predict the depth of a bed, to draw the outcrop, and to calculate true thickness and apparent dip.",
    objectives=[
        "Define a stratum contour and explain its link to strike, dip and dip direction",
        "Solve a three-point problem for strike, dip and dip direction and check it with a script-verified answer",
        "Find the depth of a bed below a point and the position of its outcrop using topographic and stratum contours",
        "Calculate true thickness from vertical thickness and apparent dip along a section line",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Stratum contour", "Structure contour", "Strike", "Dip", "Dip direction", "Three-point problem",
                  "Outcrop", "Vertical thickness", "True thickness", "Apparent dip", "V rule"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Oil and water companies drill a few boreholes and then must predict where a bed lies elsewhere. The oldest tool is a geometric one: if a bed is a flat tilted surface, three points on it fix the surface completely. The Board's practical paper tests map skills in the second year; the examiners advise problem-solving practice with evidence from the map. Here the working is always numerical, and every example in this lesson was checked by a script."),
        D("A **stratum contour** (or structure contour) is a line joining points of equal height above sea level on the surface of a bed. A stratum contour is a **strike line**: its direction is the strike of the bed. Stratum contours of a planar bed are straight, parallel and equally spaced.", title="Stratum contour"),
        KEY("What the contours tell you", "Always read three things.", cards=[
            ["Strike", "The bearing of any stratum contour (a line of equal height)."],
            ["Dip direction", "At right angles to the contours, towards the lower contour value."],
            ["Dip angle", "From the contour interval and the horizontal spacing: tan(dip) = vertical interval / horizontal distance measured at right angles to the contours."]]),
        F(r"\tan(\text{dip}) = \frac{\text{vertical interval}}{\text{horizontal distance at right angles to strike}}", title="Dip from stratum contours"),
        T("Closely spaced stratum contours mean a steep dip; widely spaced contours mean a gentle dip; equal spacing means the bed is planar. Closing contours (concentric) show a dome or a basin."),
        H("The three-point problem"),
        T("Three boreholes A, B and C reach the top of the same bed. Their positions on a map and the height of the bed in each (above sea level) are known. Find the strike, dip and dip direction of the bed, assuming it is planar."),
        IMG("algyu1-threepoint.svg", alt="Map with east and north axes in metres. Boreholes A (300 m) at the origin, B (180 m) at 1000 east, C (220 m) at 500 north. Parallel stratum contours at 300, 260, 220, 180 and 140 m run from north-west to south-east. An arrow marked dip 11 points to the north-east.", caption="Figure 1. Three-point problem and the solution: stratum contours at 40 m intervals. Heights are metres above sea level; the map is invented."),
        KEY("Method (graphical or by calculation)", "Follow these steps.", cards=[
            ["1. Join the highest and lowest points", "Join A (highest) and B (lowest) with a straight line."],
            ["2. Find the point on AB at the height of the middle borehole", "Divide AB in proportion: the height 220 m is 80/120 of the way from A (300 m) to B (180 m)."],
            ["3. Draw the strike line", "Join C to that point: this is the 220 m stratum contour."],
            ["4. Draw parallel contours", "Draw lines parallel through A and B, and at equal intervals."],
            ["5. Dip", "Measure the horizontal distance between two contours at right angles; tan(dip) = interval / distance. Dip direction is at right angles towards the lower contour."]]),
        EX("Worked example 1: three-point problem", intro="A (0, 0) is 300 m, B (1 000, 0) is 180 m and C (0, 500) is 220 m (metres east and north, heights above sea level). Find the strike, dip and dip direction.", steps=[
            S("Point on AB with height 220 m", math=r"\text{fraction} = \frac{300 - 220}{300 - 180} = \frac{2}{3}", text="The point is at 2/3 of 1 000 m: (667, 0)."),
            S("Strike", text="Join C (0, 500) to (667, 0): the line runs from north-north-west to south-east. Its bearing is **127 degrees** (also 307 degrees)."),
            S("Perpendicular distance from the 300 m contour to the 220 m contour", math=r"d = 400\text{ m}", text="A lies on the 300 m contour, 400 m from the 220 m contour measured at right angles."),
            S("Dip", math=r"\tan(\text{dip}) = \frac{300 - 220}{400} = 0.20 \Rightarrow \text{dip} = 11.3^{\circ}"),
            S("Dip direction", text="At right angles to the strike, towards the lower contours: **037 degrees** (north-east)."),
            S("Answer", text="Strike 127 degrees, dip 11 degrees towards 037 degrees (NNE)."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(0.2) = 11.31^{\circ}"),
        T("Check: the bed's height at any point is 300 - 0.12 E - 0.16 N. At A it gives 300, at B 180 and at C 220. The gradient is the square root of 0.12 squared plus 0.16 squared, which is 0.20."),
        H("Using stratum contours"),
        KEY("Applications", "These are the practical questions.", cards=[
            ["Depth to a bed", "Depth below a point = ground height - bed height at that point. If the ground height is 232 m and the bed height there is 140 m (from the stratum contour), a borehole must go 92 m deep."],
            ["Outcrop", "The bed outcrops where its stratum contour has the same height as the topographic contour. Join these intersections with a smooth line."],
            ["True thickness", "From borehole logs the vertical thickness is measured. True thickness = vertical thickness x cos(dip)."],
            ["Apparent dip", "Along a section line at an angle beta to the dip direction, tan(apparent dip) = tan(true dip) x cos(beta)."]]),
        EX("Worked example 2: depth to a seam", intro="The map above gives the top of a coal seam as the plane 300 - 0.12 E - 0.16 N. The ground height at the point (800, 400) is 232 m. How deep is the seam there, and what is its true thickness if it is 25 m thick vertically?", steps=[
            S("Height of the seam top", math=r"300 - 0.12(800) - 0.16(400) = 140\text{ m}", text="This point lies on the 140 m stratum contour."),
            S("Depth of the seam top", math=r"232 - 140 = 92\text{ m}"),
            S("Depth of the base (vertical 25 m thicker)", math=r"92 + 25 = 117\text{ m}"),
            S("True thickness", math=r"25 \times \cos 11.3^{\circ} = 24.5\text{ m}"),
        ], check=r"25 \cos(11.31^\circ) = 24.51"),
        EX("Worked example 3: apparent dip on a west-east section", intro="Using the true dip of 11.3 degrees towards 037 degrees, find the apparent dip along a section running west to east (bearing 090).", steps=[
            S("Angle between dip direction and the section", math=r"\beta = 90 - 37 = 53^{\circ}"),
            S("Formula", math=r"\tan(\text{app}) = \tan 11.3^{\circ} \times \cos 53^{\circ} = 0.20 \times 0.60 = 0.12"),
            S("Apparent dip", math=r"\text{app} = 6.8^{\circ}"),
            S("Check", text="The height falls by 0.12 m for every metre east: 120 m over 1 000 m: that is 0.12, so 6.8 degrees."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(0.12) = 6.84^{\circ}"),
        H("Contours on a map: the V rule"),
        T("When a dipping bed crosses a valley, its outcrop forms a V. The **rule of Vs** says: the V points in the direction of dip, except when the bed dips downstream more gently than the valley floor slopes; then the V points upstream. (Check the full rule in your textbook.) A vertical bed gives a straight outcrop; a horizontal bed gives an outcrop that follows the topographic contours; beds in between make Vs."),
        MISTAKE("Taking the line joining the two lowest boreholes as the strike", "The strike line joins points of EQUAL HEIGHT. Always find a point on the line joining the highest and lowest boreholes with the height of the middle one."),
        MISTAKE("Measuring the dip along the wrong line", "Measure the horizontal distance between contours at right angles to the strike. Measuring along a line at an angle gives an apparent, shallower dip."),
        MISTAKE("Quoting heights as depths", "Stratum contours give height above sea level. To find the depth below ground, subtract the bed height from the ground height."),
        QUIZ("Stratum contours on a planar bed are 200 m apart for a 40 m vertical interval. The dip is", ["5.7 degrees", "11.3 degrees", "20 degrees", "78.7 degrees"], 1, "tan(dip) = 40/200 = 0.2, so dip = 11.3 degrees.", hint="Use tan(dip) = interval / distance.", feedback=["5.7 degrees is the angle if the interval were 20 m.", "", "20 degrees would need a dip of tan 20 = 0.36.", "78.7 degrees uses 200/40 instead of 40/200."]),
        QUIZ("The strike of a bed is the same as", ["The direction of maximum slope", "The direction of a stratum contour", "The dip direction", "The direction of the fold axis"], 1, "Strike is the direction of a line of equal height on the bed: a stratum contour.", hint="Strike is horizontal.", feedback=["That is the dip direction, at 90 degrees to strike.", "", "Dip direction is at right angles.", "The fold axis is a different line."]),
        TIP("Stratum contours, three-point problems, true thickness and apparent dip appear in Paper 3 (practical, with maps) and in numerical parts of Paper 1. Work neatly, label the contour heights and show the right-angle measurement; the Board advises regular practice of map work and problem-solving based on evidence from the map.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("Define a stratum contour and state what its direction gives.", "A stratum contour is a line joining points of equal height above sea level on the surface of a bed. Its direction is the strike of the bed."),
        PQ("Boreholes P (0, 0), Q (600, 0) and R (0, 300) meet the top of a limestone at 240 m, 120 m and 180 m. Find the dip and the strike.", "Height 180 m is 60/120 = half-way from P to Q: the point (300, 0). The strike line joins R (0, 300) to (300, 0): north-west to south-east (bearing 135 degrees). The perpendicular distance from this line to P is 300 x 300 / sqrt(300^2 + 300^2) = 212.1 m, for a vertical drop of 60 m. Dip = arctan(60/212.1) = 15.8 degrees towards the north-east (045 degrees)."),
        PQ("A sandstone bed is 30 m thick in a vertical borehole and dips at 20 degrees. Calculate its true thickness.", "True thickness = 30 x cos 20 degrees = 30 x 0.940 = 28.2 m."),
        PQ("A bed dips at 30 degrees towards the north. Calculate its apparent dip along a section running north-east.", "beta = 45 degrees. tan(app) = tan 30 x cos 45 = 0.5774 x 0.7071 = 0.408; apparent dip = 22.2 degrees."),
        SUM("A stratum contour joins points of equal height on a bed; its direction is the strike.",
            "Dip is at right angles to the contours: tan(dip) = interval / distance at right angles.",
            "Three-point problem: find the middle height on the line from highest to lowest borehole, join to the middle borehole for the strike, then calculate the dip.",
            "Depth = ground height - bed height; the outcrop is where the contours have equal values.",
            "True thickness = vertical thickness x cos(dip); tan(apparent dip) = tan(dip) x cos(beta)."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 14. Geological cross-sections
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Geological cross-sections",
    title="Geological cross-sections: drawing and interpreting",
    slug="gyu1-geological-cross-sections",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Draw a geological section from a map along a line: mark contacts, find apparent dips, join beds into folds, insert axes and core beds, state vertical exaggeration and compute depths. Interpret a section in terms of structure and geological history.",
    objectives=[
        "Draw a geological cross-section along a line on a map with the beds, fold axes and core beds",
        "Calculate the apparent dip along an oblique section and the dip drawn at a vertical exaggeration",
        "Calculate depths of contacts and the true thickness from a section",
        "Interpret a section to describe the structure and place events in order",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Cross-section", "Apparent dip", "Vertical exaggeration", "Topographic profile", "Contact", "Axial trace",
                  "Core bed", "Outlier", "Inlier", "Geological history"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A geological map shows what is at the surface; a geological section shows what lies underneath, as if the ground had been cut by a vertical knife along a line. In the Board's 2023 practical paper, most candidates could not draw the section across the line XY. Some redrew the map and labelled it; some drew only the topographic profile; many did not insert the beds, the fold axes and the core beds. This lesson gives a method that avoids those errors."),
        D("A **geological cross-section** is a vertical slice through the ground showing the topographic profile and the rock units and structures beneath it. It must have a title, the two end points (X and Y, with the compass directions), a horizontal and vertical scale, the **vertical exaggeration**, labelled rock units, and the structures (fold axes, faults).", title="Cross-section"),
        D("**Vertical exaggeration** (VE) = horizontal scale / vertical scale, written as VE = 2 or x2. At VE = 1 the section is true to scale. Exaggeration steepens all dips on the drawing, so a stated true dip of 30 degrees is drawn steeper.", title="Vertical exaggeration", formula=r"VE = \frac{\text{horizontal scale}}{\text{vertical scale}} \qquad \tan\delta_{drawn} = VE \times \tan\delta"),
        H("The method"),
        KEY("Eight steps", "Follow them in order on the exam paper.", cards=[
            ["1. Mark the line", "Draw the section line XY on a strip of paper and mark each contact, fault and fold axis where the line crosses it."],
            ["2. Topographic profile", "Mark the ground height at each point (from contours) and draw the ground surface. On a map with flat ground it is a straight line."],
            ["3. Transfer the contacts", "Place the marks on the profile at the right horizontal distances."],
            ["4. Add dips", "From the dip symbols, draw short lines at the correct dip. If the section line is not at right angles to the strike, calculate the **apparent dip**."],
            ["5. Join the contacts", "Join matching contacts with smooth or straight lines, following the dips, so the same bed continues under the ground as an arch (anticline) or trough (syncline)."],
            ["6. Fill and label", "Fill each unit and label it. Mark the fold axes, the core beds and any faults with arrows for relative movement."],
            ["7. Thickness", "Each bed keeps its true thickness around a parallel fold; check that the widths you drew agree with the thickness and dip."],
            ["8. Add the title", "Title, scale, VE, end points X and Y with directions, and a key if needed. A key alone is not a section."]]),
        IMG("algyu1-section.svg", alt="Cross-section X to Y of folded beds: C in the west, B, then A in the core of an anticline, then B and a wide trough of C in the syncline, then B and A at the east end. Dashed lines show the eroded arch and the fold axes. VE = 2.", caption="Figure 1. Section along XY in the map of the Folds lesson. The vertical axis is height in kilometres; VE = 2."),
        EX("Worked example 1: apparent dip along an oblique section", intro="Beds strike north to south and dip at 30 degrees. A section is drawn along a line running at 060 degrees. What apparent dip should be drawn (VE = 1)?", steps=[
            S("Angle beta between the section and the strike", math=r"\beta = 60^{\circ}", text="The strike is 000 degrees; the line is 60 degrees from it."),
            S("Formula", math=r"\tan(\text{app}) = \tan\delta \times \sin\beta"),
            S("Calculate", math=r"\tan(\text{app}) = 0.5774 \times 0.8660 = 0.5"),
            S("Apparent dip", math=r"\text{app} = 26.6^{\circ}"),
            S("Check", text="A section at right angles to strike (beta = 90 degrees) shows the true dip of 30 degrees; a section along the strike (beta = 0) shows 0."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(0.5) = 26.57^{\circ}"),
        T("Note the two formulae: tan(app) = tan(dip) x sin(beta) uses the angle beta to the **strike**; tan(app) = tan(dip) x cos(alpha) uses the angle alpha to the **dip direction**. They give the same answer because alpha = 90 - beta."),
        EX("Worked example 2: vertical exaggeration", intro="In the section of Figure 1 (VE = 2), the beds dip at 30 degrees. At what angle are they drawn?", steps=[
            S("Formula", math=r"\tan\delta_{drawn} = VE \times \tan\delta"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"\tan\delta_{drawn} = 2 \times \tan 30^{\circ} = 2 \times 0.5774 = 1.155"),
            S("Angle drawn", math=r"\delta_{drawn} = 49.1^{\circ}"),
            S("Comment", text="The drawn dip is steeper than the real dip. Always say the VE on the section so readers do not take the drawn dips as true."),
        ], check=r"\tan^{-1}(1.155) = 49.1^{\circ}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: depth to a contact from the map", intro="In the map of the Folds lesson the beds dip at 30 degrees, the anticline axis is at easting 43 and the syncline axis at easting 46. Bed B is 0.40 km thick (true thickness). At the anticline axis the top of bed A would be 0.30 km above the flat ground. How deep below the ground is the top of A at the syncline axis, and the top of B (the base of C)?", steps=[
            S("Horizontal distance from the anticline axis to the syncline axis", math=r"46 - 43 = 3\text{ km}"),
            S("Drop along a 30 degree dip", math=r"3 \times \tan 30^{\circ} = 1.732\text{ km}"),
            S("Height of the top of A at the syncline axis", math=r"0.30 - 1.732 = -1.432\text{ km}"),
            S("Depth of the top of A", text="1.43 km (1 432 m) below the ground."),
            S("Vertical thickness of B", math=r"\frac{0.40}{\cos 30^{\circ}} = 0.462\text{ km}"),
            S("Depth of the top of B (base of C)", math=r"1.432 - 0.462 = 0.970\text{ km}"),
        ], check=r"1.432 - 0.462 = 0.970"),
        H("Interpreting a section: geological history"),
        T("Read a section like a story. Older beds lie below younger ones unless the section is overturned. Where beds are cut by a fault or intrusion, the fault or intrusion is younger. An **unconformity** (a surface of erosion separating older from younger beds) records uplift and erosion. Describe the events in order from oldest to youngest: deposition of A, B and C; folding (compression); erosion to a level surface."),
        KEY("Outliers and inliers", "The Board's examiners expected an outlier to be identified from map evidence.", cards=[
            ["Outlier", "A small area of younger rock completely surrounded by older rock. It is formed when folding or faulting preserved the younger bed, and erosion removed the rest. The evidence: a limited outcrop of younger rock entirely surrounded by older rock."],
            ["Inlier", "A small area of older rock completely surrounded by younger rock, usually exposed by erosion through a dome or anticline."],
            ["Common error", "The Board's report notes candidates who called an outlier an 'angular unconformity' or a rock type, or who explained it by deposition, volcanic eruption or weathering instead of erosion and folding."]]),
        MISTAKE("Drawing the topographic profile and calling it the section", "The topographic profile is only the top line of a section. A geological section must show the beds, fold axes and core beds below it."),
        MISTAKE("Redrawing the map", "A section is a vertical slice, not a plan. Do not copy the map outlines; mark the contacts along the line and project them downwards."),
        MISTAKE("Using the true dip on an oblique section", "Along a line not at right angles to strike the beds appear to dip more gently. Calculate the apparent dip, and use VE if the vertical scale is different."),
        QUIZ("Beds dip at 40 degrees. On a section drawn at the true scale but along the strike, the dip drawn is", ["40 degrees", "0 degrees", "20 degrees", "90 degrees"], 1, "Along the strike the beds are level: apparent dip = tan(dip) x sin(0) = 0.", hint="What is the height change along a strike line?", feedback=["40 degrees is the true dip, shown only on a section at right angles to strike.", "", "20 degrees would need an angle beta of about 36 degrees.", "Dips of 90 degrees need vertical beds."]),
        QUIZ("A section has a horizontal scale of 1:50 000 and a vertical scale of 1:25 000. Its vertical exaggeration is", ["0.5", "1", "2", "25 000"], 2, "VE = 50 000/25 000 = 2.", hint="VE is horizontal scale divided by vertical scale.", feedback=["That inverts the ratio.", "A VE of 1 means the scales are equal.", "", "That is the vertical scale denominator."]),
        TIP("Drawing a section across a line on a map is part of the map question of Paper 3, and numerical parts (apparent dip, VE, depth) can appear in Paper 1. The Board's examiners wanted the beds, fold axes and core beds on the section, and warned against drawing only a topographic profile or redrawing the map.", title="Exam link"),
        PQ("List the information that a complete geological cross-section should show.", "A title; the end points of the section line (X and Y) with directions; horizontal scale and vertical scale or VE; the topographic profile; the rock units labelled with their boundaries (contacts) and dips; fold axes and core beds; faults with relative movement; and a key if needed."),
        PQ("Beds strike 000 degrees and dip 50 degrees east. Calculate the apparent dip on a section running at 045 degrees (VE = 1).", "Beta (angle to strike) = 45 degrees. tan(app) = tan 50 x sin 45 = 1.1918 x 0.7071 = 0.8427. App = 40.1 degrees."),
        PQ("A section is drawn with VE = 3. What angle is drawn for a bed dipping at 30 degrees?", "tan(drawn) = 3 x tan 30 = 1.732, so the drawn dip is 60 degrees."),
        PQ("Describe the geological history shown by Figure 1.", "Beds A, B and C were deposited in that order (oldest to youngest). They were then compressed and folded into an anticline and a syncline with axes trending north to south and 30 degree limb dips. Uplift and erosion followed, removing the crest of the anticline (dashed) and exposing A in its core and C in the core of the syncline."),
        SUM("A cross-section is a vertical slice with title, end points, scales, VE, labelled units, fold axes and core beds.",
            "Method: mark contacts along the line, draw the topographic profile, add dips, join contacts, label, add axes and thickness.",
            "Apparent dip: tan(app) = tan(dip) x sin(beta), beta measured from the strike.",
            "Vertical exaggeration: VE = horizontal scale / vertical scale; drawn dip: tan(drawn) = VE x tan(dip).",
            "Do not draw only the topographic profile or redraw the map.",
            "Outliers (younger rock surrounded by older) form by folding or faulting plus erosion."),
    ],
))

# ==== END OF LESSONS ====
